ForgeData.register("phys",{"PHYS-1":{"label":"Mechanics","color":"#1d4ed8","questions":[{"id":"PHYS-01","stem":"A student writes: 'A car travelling at constant velocity has no forces acting on it.' Why is this wrong?","options":{"A":"Constant velocity means the resultant force is zero, not that no forces act","B":"Constant velocity means the car is accelerating.","C":"Scalars and vectors are interchangeable — the distinction doesn't matter in calculations.","D":"Forces only act when objects are stationary."},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GPHYS-RESULTANT-FORCE","scaffold":"Newton's First Law: an object continues at constant velocity (or rest) when the resultant force is zero. This does NOT mean no forces act — it means forces are balanced. A car at constant speed has: driving force (engine) balanced by friction and air resistance. Students confuse 'no resultant force' with 'no forces.'","reforge":{"stem":"A skydiver falls at terminal velocity. Which statement correctly describes the forces?","options":{"A":"No forces act on the skydiver.","B":"An external gravitational field acting on it under standard conditions in a typical experiment","C":"Weight equals air resistance — the resultant force is zero so velocity is constant.","D":"Air resistance is greater than weight."},"correct":"C"},"spec":"PHYS-1","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-phys-3.4"},{"id":"PHYS-02","stem":"A ball is thrown horizontally from a cliff. Ignoring air resistance, which correctly describes its motion?","options":{"A":"It moves in a straight line diagonally downward.","B":"Horizontal velocity remains constant","C":"Horizontal and vertical motion cannot be analysed separately.","D":"It decelerates horizontally because gravity pulls it back."},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GPHYS-MOTION-GRAVITY","scaffold":"Projectile motion: horizontal and vertical components are independent. Horizontal: no force (ignoring air resistance) → constant velocity. Vertical: gravitational force → constant acceleration downward (g = 9.81 m/s²). Combined: parabolic trajectory. Common error: students think horizontal velocity decreases due to gravity — gravity only acts vertically.","reforge":{"stem":"A projectile is launched at 30° to the horizontal with speed 20 m/s. What is the initial vertical component of velocity?","options":{"A":"20 m/s","B":"They should have used v = u + at instead.","C":"17.3 m/s","D":"10 m/s (20 × sin30° = 20 × 0.5 = 10 m/s)"},"correct":"D"},"spec":"PHYS-1","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-phys-3.5"},{"id":"PHYS-03","stem":"Which of the following correctly states the work-energy theorem?","options":{"A":"Parallel circuits always have lower resistance than series circuits regardless of component values.","B":"Work done equals the force applied multiplied by time.","C":"The net work done on an object equals the change in its kinetic energy: W_net = ΔKE = ½mv²","D":"Work equals power multiplied by force."},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GPHYS-WORK-ENERGY","scaffold":"Work-energy theorem: W_net = ΔKE. Work done = force × displacement (in direction of force): W = Fd cosθ. If net work is positive, KE increases; if negative, KE decreases. Distinct from conservation of energy (which also includes PE). Common errors: confusing work with impulse (Ft), or assuming work = ΔPE only.","reforge":{"stem":"A 2 kg object accelerates from 3 m/s to 7 m/s. What is the net work done on it?","options":{"A":"40 J (ΔKE = ½×2×7² − ½×2×3² = 49 − 9 = 40 J)","B":"8 J","C":"Work done is always equal to the gain in potential energy.","D":"80 J"},"correct":"A"},"spec":"PHYS-1","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-phys-3.4"},{"id":"PHYS-04","stem":"A student uses s = ut + ½at² to find displacement but gets the wrong answer because they forgot to check something. What is the most likely error?","options":{"A":"They forgot to square t.","B":"They should have used v = u + at instead.","C":"SUVAT equations only work for horizontal motion.","D":"They used inconsistent sign conventions"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GPHYS-MOTION-GRAVITY","scaffold":"SUVAT equations require consistent sign conventions throughout the calculation. Choose a positive direction (usually up or right) and stick to it. If upward is positive: initial upward velocity = +u, g = −9.81 m/s². If a ball is thrown upward and comes back, displacement is eventually negative (below start). Mixing signs is the single most common SUVAT error in AQA mark schemes.","reforge":{"stem":"A ball is thrown upward at 15 m/s. Taking upward as positive, what is its displacement after 4 seconds? (g = 9.81 m/s²)","options":{"A":"60 m","B":"−18.5 m","C":"18.5 m above the start","D":"0 m"},"correct":"B"},"spec":"PHYS-1","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-phys-3.1"},{"id":"PHYS-05","stem":"Hooke's Law states that F = kx. A student says: 'This means a stiffer spring stores more energy.' Is this always correct?","options":{"A":"Not necessarily. Elastic potential energy = ½kx²","B":"Hooke's Law doesn't apply to energy calculations.","C":"Yes — higher k always means more energy stored.","D":"Stiffer springs always store less energy."},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GPHYS-WORK-ENERGY","scaffold":"EPE = ½kx² = F²/(2k). For the same extension: stiffer spring stores more energy (higher k). For the same force: stiffer spring extends less, stores less energy (F²/2k decreases as k increases). The question of 'which stores more' depends on whether you fix force or extension. AQA examiners test this distinction.","reforge":{"stem":"Spring A has k = 200 N/m, Spring B has k = 50 N/m. Both are extended by 0.1 m. Which stores more elastic potential energy?","options":{"A":"Spring B stores more.","B":"Both store equal energy.","C":"Spring A stores more, being stiffer","D":"Cannot be determined without knowing the force."},"correct":"C"},"spec":"PHYS-1","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-phys-3.4"},{"id":"PHYS-06","stem":"The principle of conservation of momentum states that:","options":{"A":"Momentum is always conserved in elastic collisions only.","B":"The total momentum of a closed system remains constant provided no external resultant force acts","C":"Momentum is conserved only when objects stick together.","D":"The higher-frequency light increases the wavelength of the incident beam under standard test conditions"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GPHYS-RESULTANT-FORCE","scaffold":"Momentum conservation: applies to ALL collisions (elastic and inelastic) in a closed system (no external net force). Kinetic energy is only conserved in perfectly elastic collisions. In inelastic collisions, KE is lost as heat/sound/deformation — but momentum is always conserved. Students frequently confuse momentum conservation with KE conservation.","reforge":{"stem":"Two objects collide and stick together (perfectly inelastic collision). Which quantities are conserved?","options":{"A":"It is reflected straight back along the normal under standard test conditions in a typical experiment","B":"Neither is conserved.","C":"Kinetic energy only.","D":"Momentum only — kinetic energy is lost in an inelastic collision as heat, sound, or deformation."},"correct":"D"},"spec":"PHYS-1","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-phys-3.4"},{"id":"PHYS-07","stem":"A student confuses 'scalar' and 'vector' quantities. Which list correctly separates them?","options":{"A":"Scalars and vectors are interchangeable — the distinction doesn't matter in calculations.","B":"Scalars: velocity, force, acceleration. Vectors: speed, distance, energy.","C":"Scalars: speed, distance, mass, energy, temperature. Vectors: velocity","D":"All physical quantities are vectors."},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GPHYS-SCALAR-VECTOR","scaffold":"Scalars have magnitude only (speed, distance, mass, energy, time, temperature, pressure). Vectors have magnitude AND direction (velocity, displacement, force, acceleration, momentum, weight). Practical implication: two forces of 5 N can give a resultant of anything from 0 N to 10 N depending on direction. Adding scalars uses arithmetic; adding vectors requires geometry (parallelogram or component method).","reforge":{"stem":"A car travels 3 km north then 4 km east. Its total distance travelled is 7 km. What is the magnitude of its displacement?","options":{"A":"5 km (displacement is a vector — use Pythagoras: √(3²+4²) = 5 km at a bearing NE)","B":"7 km","C":"1 km","D":"It is constant and always directed away from equilibrium under standard conditions"},"correct":"A"},"spec":"PHYS-1","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-phys-3.4"},{"id":"PHYS-08","stem":"Which correctly explains why a heavier object and a lighter object dropped from the same height hit the ground at the same time (ignoring air resistance)?","options":{"A":"The heavier object has more gravitational force pulling it, so it falls faster.","B":"Objects in free fall have no weight.","C":"Air resistance makes the difference negligible.","D":"Gravitational acceleration (g) is independent of mass"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GPHYS-MOTION-GRAVITY","scaffold":"F = mg. Newton's 2nd law: a = F/m = mg/m = g. Mass cancels — acceleration is independent of mass. This is Galileo's insight. Heavier object: more force BUT more inertia to overcome. The ratio is always g. Air resistance does differentiate them in reality (depends on cross-section and mass), but in a vacuum, ALL objects fall identically regardless of mass.","reforge":{"stem":"On the Moon, where g = 1.6 m/s², a 1 kg and a 10 kg object are dropped simultaneously. Which hits the ground first?","options":{"A":"The 10 kg object — more gravitational force.","B":"They hit simultaneously, whatever their mass","C":"The 1 kg object — less inertia.","D":"Cannot be determined without knowing the height."},"correct":"B"},"spec":"PHYS-1","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-phys-3.5"},{"id":"PHYS-E1-01","spec":"PHYS-1","stem":"The gradient of a displacement-time graph represents:","options":{"A":"velocity","B":"acceleration","C":"force","D":"momentum"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GPHYS-GRAPHS-KINEMATICS","scaffold":"The rate of change of displacement with time is velocity.","reforge":{"stem":"On a graph of displacement against time, what physical quantity does the gradient at any point represent?","options":{"A":"The velocity at that point","B":"The acceleration at that point","C":"The force acting at that point","D":"The momentum at that point"},"correct":"A"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-phys-3.4"},{"id":"PHYS-E1-02","spec":"PHYS-1","stem":"A resultant force of zero means an object:","options":{"A":"must be accelerating","B":"has constant velocity or remains at rest","C":"Constant velocity means the car is accelerating.","D":"must be moving in a circle"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GPHYS-RESULTANT-FORCE","scaffold":"Forces may act but cancel; Newton's first law gives zero acceleration.","reforge":{"stem":"A crate sits still on the floor with all forces acting on it perfectly balanced. What does a resultant force of zero tell you about its motion?","options":{"A":"It must be accelerating","B":"It is either at rest or moving at constant velocity","C":"No forces at all are acting on it under standard conditions","D":"It must be moving in a circle"},"correct":"B"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-phys-3.4"},{"id":"PHYS-E1-03","spec":"PHYS-1","stem":"The work done by a force is zero when the force is:","options":{"A":"Work done equals the force applied multiplied by time.","B":"larger than the weight","C":"perpendicular to the displacement","D":"acting for one second"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GPHYS-WORK-ENERGY","scaffold":"Work is F s cos θ, so a right angle gives zero work by that force.","reforge":{"stem":"A satellite moves in a circular orbit, with gravity always acting directly towards the centre of its path. Why does gravity do no work on it?","options":{"A":"Because the force is parallel to the displacement","B":"Because the force is larger than the satellite's weight under standard conditions","C":"Because the force only acts for one second","D":"Because the force is perpendicular to the direction of motion"},"correct":"D"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-phys-3.4"},{"id":"PHYS-E1-04","spec":"PHYS-1","stem":"The SI unit of electric current is:","options":{"A":"volt","B":"ohm","C":"coulomb","D":"ampere"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GPHYS-CIRCUITS","scaffold":"Current is measured in amperes; charge is measured in coulombs.","reforge":{"stem":"Which SI base unit measures electric current?","options":{"A":"The ohm","B":"The ampere","C":"The coulomb","D":"The volt"},"correct":"A"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-phys-3.5"},{"id":"PHYS-E1-05","spec":"PHYS-1","stem":"A material obeys Hooke's law while:","options":{"A":"extension is proportional to force","B":"stress is always zero","C":"it has no elastic limit","D":"Inversely proportional to the force"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GPHYS-SPRINGS-MATERIALS","scaffold":"The proportional region ends when the force-extension graph stops being linear.","reforge":{"stem":"A spring is stretched by increasing amounts, and a graph of force against extension gives a straight line through the origin. What does this straight-line region show?","options":{"A":"The spring has already passed its elastic limit under standard conditions","B":"The spring is obeying Hooke's law, with extension proportional to force","C":"The spring's stress is always zero","D":"The spring's mass is proportional to its length"},"correct":"B"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-phys-3.4"},{"id":"PHYS-E1-06","spec":"PHYS-1","stem":"The half-life of a radioactive isotope is:","options":{"A":"Light ejects electrons more quickly from metals than from non-metals.","B":"the time for the activity or number of undecayed nuclei to halve","C":"the time for the mass to double","D":"the time for radiation to stop"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GPHYS-QUANTUM-NUCLEAR","scaffold":"Radioactive decay is random for individual nuclei but predictable statistically.","reforge":{"stem":"A sample of a radioactive isotope is measured, and after a certain fixed time, only half of the original undecayed nuclei remain. What is this fixed time called?","options":{"A":"The time for every nucleus in the sample to decay under standard conditions","B":"The time for the sample's mass to double","C":"The time for all radiation to stop completely","D":"The half-life, the time for the number of undecayed nuclei to halve"},"correct":"D"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-phys-3.8"},{"id":"PHYS-N1-01","stem":"Which quantity is a vector rather than a scalar?","options":{"A":"Mass","B":"Speed","C":"Velocity","D":"Energy stored in a system"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GPHYS-SCALAR-VECTOR","scaffold":"Scalars have magnitude only: mass, speed, distance, energy, temperature, time. Vectors have magnitude and direction: velocity, displacement, force, acceleration, momentum. The pairs matter — distance is scalar while displacement is vector, and speed is scalar while velocity is vector. A body moving in a complete circle has travelled a distance but has zero displacement, and so zero average velocity.","reforge":{"stem":"A runner completes one lap of a 400 m track. Their displacement is:","options":{"A":"0 m","B":"200 m","C":"400 m","D":"800 m"},"correct":"A"},"spec":"PHYS-1","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-phys-3.4"},{"id":"PHYS-N1-02","stem":"Two forces of 3 N and 4 N act at right angles. The magnitude of the resultant is:","options":{"A":"7 N","B":"1 N","C":"12 N","D":"5 N"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GPHYS-SCALAR-VECTOR","scaffold":"Perpendicular vectors combine by Pythagoras: √(3² + 4²) = √25 = 5 N. Adding magnitudes directly (7 N) is only valid when the vectors are parallel and in the same direction; subtracting (1 N) applies when they are antiparallel. The direction of the resultant is found with tan θ = opposite ÷ adjacent, here tan θ = 4/3, giving 53° from the 3 N force.","reforge":{"stem":"A force of 20 N acts at 30° to the horizontal. Its horizontal component is:","options":{"A":"20 ÷ cos 30° = 23.1 N","B":"20 cos 30° = 17.3 N","C":"20 tan 30° = 11.5 N","D":"20 sin 30° = 10 N"},"correct":"B"},"spec":"PHYS-1","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-phys-3.4"},{"id":"PHYS-N1-03","stem":"The gradient of a velocity–time graph represents:","options":{"A":"Acceleration","B":"Distance travelled","C":"Displacement","D":"Momentum"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GPHYS-GRAPHS-KINEMATICS","scaffold":"On a displacement–time graph the gradient gives velocity. On a velocity–time graph the gradient gives acceleration and the area beneath gives displacement. On an acceleration–time graph the area beneath gives change in velocity. Reading the area where the gradient is wanted, or the reverse, is the standard error — check which quantity the axes actually carry before deciding.","reforge":{"stem":"The area under a velocity–time graph represents:","options":{"A":"Acceleration","B":"Force applied","C":"Displacement","D":"Power"},"correct":"C"},"spec":"PHYS-1","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-phys-3.4"},{"id":"PHYS-N1-04","stem":"A car accelerates uniformly from rest at 3 m s⁻² for 8 s. Its final velocity is:","options":{"A":"96 m s⁻¹","B":"24 m s⁻¹","C":"11 m s⁻¹","D":"0.375 m s⁻¹"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GPHYS-GRAPHS-KINEMATICS","scaffold":"Use v = u + at with u = 0, a = 3 and t = 8, giving v = 0 + 24 = 24 m s⁻¹. The four suvat equations apply only to uniform acceleration. List the known quantities first, then choose the equation containing exactly one unknown. Taking a consistent sign convention matters when motion reverses direction, since a and v may then have opposite signs.","reforge":{"stem":"A ball is dropped from rest. Using g = 9.81 m s⁻², how far does it fall in 2.0 s?","options":{"A":"9.8 m","B":"4.9 m","C":"39.2 m","D":"19.6 m"},"correct":"D"},"spec":"PHYS-1","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-phys-3.4"},{"id":"PHYS-N1-05","stem":"An object is in equilibrium. This means the resultant force on it is:","options":{"A":"Increasing steadily","B":"Equal to its weight","C":"Zero, so it has no acceleration","D":"Equal to the object's total weight"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GPHYS-RESULTANT-FORCE","scaffold":"Equilibrium means zero resultant force and zero resultant moment, so the object has no acceleration — it is either at rest or moving at constant velocity. Newton's first law makes the same point: a body continues in its state of motion unless acted on by a resultant force. Constant velocity therefore does not imply zero force, only that the forces present balance.","reforge":{"stem":"A skydiver falls at constant velocity. The forces on them are:","options":{"A":"Balanced, giving zero resultant","B":"Drag greater than weight","C":"Weight greater than drag","D":"Zero, since there is no acceleration"},"correct":"A"},"spec":"PHYS-1","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-phys-3.4"},{"id":"PHYS-N1-06","stem":"A resultant force of 12 N acts on a 4 kg mass. Its acceleration is:","options":{"A":"48 m s⁻²","B":"0.33 m s⁻²","C":"8 m s⁻²","D":"3 m s⁻²"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GPHYS-RESULTANT-FORCE","scaffold":"Newton's second law gives F = ma, so a = F ÷ m = 12 ÷ 4 = 3 m s⁻². The force used must be the resultant, not one individual force — if several forces act, combine them first. Mass is in kilograms and force in newtons; using grams gives an answer a thousand times too large. Weight is a force, W = mg, and differs from mass, which is a scalar measured in kg.","reforge":{"stem":"An object of mass 5.0 kg is on Earth where g = 9.81 N kg⁻¹. Its weight is:","options":{"A":"9.81 N","B":"49 N","C":"0.51 N","D":"5.0 N"},"correct":"B"},"spec":"PHYS-1","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-phys-3.4"},{"id":"PHYS-N1-07","stem":"Newton's third law states that forces occur in pairs which are equal in magnitude, opposite in direction and:","options":{"A":"Act on different objects","B":"Always cancel to zero","C":"Act on the same single object","D":"Are of different types"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GPHYS-RESULTANT-FORCE","scaffold":"A third-law pair acts on two different bodies, is of the same type, and the two forces are equal and opposite. Because they act on different objects they never cancel each other — cancelling only happens between forces on the same body. A book on a table: the book's weight and the table's normal contact force are not a third-law pair, since both act on the book; they happen to balance because the book is in equilibrium.","reforge":{"stem":"A book rests on a table. The Newton's third law pair to the book's weight is the:","options":{"A":"Friction between the book and the table surface","B":"Weight of the table","C":"Gravitational pull of the book on the Earth","D":"Friction between book and table"},"correct":"C"},"spec":"PHYS-1","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-phys-3.4"},{"id":"PHYS-N1-08","stem":"The moment of a force about a point is calculated as:","options":{"A":"Force × time","B":"Force × perpendicular distance","C":"Force × mass","D":"Force divided by the distance from the pivot"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GPHYS-RESULTANT-FORCE","scaffold":"Moment = force × perpendicular distance from the pivot, measured in N m. The distance must be perpendicular to the line of action, which is why a force applied along a line through the pivot has zero moment. The principle of moments states that for equilibrium the sum of clockwise moments equals the sum of anticlockwise moments about any point.","reforge":{"stem":"A 30 N force acts 0.4 m from a pivot, perpendicular to the bar. Its moment is:","options":{"A":"75 N m","B":"0.013 N m","C":"30.4 N m","D":"12 N m"},"correct":"D"},"spec":"PHYS-1","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-phys-3.4"},{"id":"PHYS-N1-09","stem":"A uniform beam balances on a pivot. A 20 N weight sits 0.6 m to the left. A weight 0.4 m to the right must be:","options":{"A":"20 N","B":"12 N","C":"30 N","D":"8 N"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GPHYS-RESULTANT-FORCE","scaffold":"Apply the principle of moments: clockwise = anticlockwise, so 20 × 0.6 = F × 0.4, giving 12 = 0.4F and F = 30 N. The nearer the pivot, the larger the force needed. For a uniform beam the weight acts at its centre, which must be included when the beam is not pivoted at its midpoint. Taking moments about the pivot removes the unknown pivot reaction from the calculation.","reforge":{"stem":"Taking moments about the pivot is convenient because the pivot's reaction force has:","options":{"A":"Zero moment about that point","B":"A very large moment","C":"No magnitude at all","D":"A very large moment about that point"},"correct":"A"},"spec":"PHYS-1","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-phys-3.4"},{"id":"PHYS-N1-10","stem":"Work done by a constant force is calculated as:","options":{"A":"Force × time","B":"Force × mass","C":"Force multiplied by the time for which it acts","D":"Force × distance along the force"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GPHYS-WORK-ENERGY","scaffold":"Work done W = Fs cos θ, where θ is the angle between the force and the displacement, measured in joules. When the force is perpendicular to the motion, cos 90° = 0 and no work is done — which is why the centripetal force in circular motion does no work and the speed stays constant. Work done equals energy transferred, so the two are always measured in the same unit.","reforge":{"stem":"A force acts perpendicular to an object's motion. The work done by that force is:","options":{"A":"Equal to Fs","B":"Zero","C":"Negative","D":"Half of Fs"},"correct":"B"},"spec":"PHYS-1","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-phys-3.4"},{"id":"PHYS-N1-11","stem":"A 2.0 kg object moves at 6.0 m s⁻¹. Its kinetic energy is:","options":{"A":"36 J","B":"6.0 J","C":"12 J","D":"72 J"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GPHYS-WORK-ENERGY","scaffold":"Kinetic energy = ½mv² = ½ × 2.0 × 36 = 36 J. Note the velocity is squared, so doubling the speed quadruples the kinetic energy — forgetting to square gives 6 J, and forgetting the half gives 72 J. Gravitational potential energy near the Earth's surface is mgΔh. In the absence of resistive forces the sum of the two is conserved.","reforge":{"stem":"An object of mass 3.0 kg is raised 2.0 m. Using g = 9.81 N kg⁻¹, the gain in potential energy is:","options":{"A":"29.4 J","B":"6.0 J","C":"58.9 J","D":"1.5 J"},"correct":"C"},"spec":"PHYS-1","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-phys-3.4"},{"id":"PHYS-N1-12","stem":"Power is best defined as:","options":{"A":"The total energy transferred","B":"Energy transferred per unit time","C":"Force multiplied by distance moved","D":"Force multiplied by distance"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GPHYS-WORK-ENERGY","scaffold":"Power = energy transferred ÷ time, measured in watts, where one watt is one joule per second. For a body moving at steady speed against a resistive force, P = Fv, since power is the rate of doing work. Efficiency = useful output ÷ total input, expressed as a decimal or percentage, and can never exceed 1 — an answer above 100% signals an arithmetic error.","reforge":{"stem":"A motor takes 500 J of input energy and delivers 350 J usefully. Its efficiency is:","options":{"A":"143%","B":"150%","C":"30%","D":"70%"},"correct":"D"},"spec":"PHYS-1","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-phys-3.4"},{"id":"PHYS-N1-13","stem":"The principle of conservation of momentum applies when:","options":{"A":"Kinetic energy is also conserved","B":"The collision is perfectly elastic and nothing else","C":"No external resultant force acts on the system","D":"The objects have equal mass"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GPHYS-MOTION-GRAVITY","scaffold":"Momentum p = mv is conserved in any collision or explosion provided no external resultant force acts, whether the collision is elastic or inelastic. Kinetic energy is conserved only in an elastic collision; in an inelastic one some becomes internal energy or sound, though momentum is still conserved. Momentum is a vector, so direction matters and a consistent sign convention must be chosen.","reforge":{"stem":"In an inelastic collision, which quantity is not conserved?","options":{"A":"Kinetic energy","B":"Total energy of the system","C":"Momentum","D":"Mass"},"correct":"A"},"spec":"PHYS-1","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-phys-3.4"},{"id":"PHYS-N1-14","stem":"A 2.0 kg trolley moving at 3.0 m s⁻¹ collides and sticks to a stationary 1.0 kg trolley. Their common velocity is:","options":{"A":"3.0 m s⁻¹","B":"6.0 m s⁻¹","C":"1.5 m s⁻¹","D":"2.0 m s⁻¹"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GPHYS-MOTION-GRAVITY","scaffold":"Conserve momentum: total before = (2.0 × 3.0) + (1.0 × 0) = 6.0 kg m s⁻¹. After the collision the combined mass is 3.0 kg, so v = 6.0 ÷ 3.0 = 2.0 m s⁻¹. The objects stick together, so this is an inelastic collision and kinetic energy falls from 9.0 J to 6.0 J, the difference becoming internal energy and sound.","reforge":{"stem":"Impulse is equal to:","options":{"A":"Force × distance","B":"Force × time, and to Δmomentum","C":"Mass multiplied by acceleration produced","D":"Force ÷ time"},"correct":"B"},"spec":"PHYS-1","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-phys-3.4"},{"id":"PHYS-N1-15","stem":"For a spring obeying Hooke's law, the extension is:","options":{"A":"Directly proportional to the force applied","B":"Independent of the force","C":"Inversely proportional to the force","D":"Inversely proportional to the applied force"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GPHYS-SPRINGS-MATERIALS","scaffold":"Hooke's law gives F = kΔL, so extension is proportional to force up to the limit of proportionality. Beyond that the graph curves; past the elastic limit the material no longer returns to its original length. The gradient of a force–extension graph is the spring constant k in N m⁻¹, and the area beneath it is the elastic strain energy stored, ½FΔL or ½kΔL².","reforge":{"stem":"A spring of stiffness 200 N m⁻¹ is extended by 0.10 m. The energy stored is:","options":{"A":"20 J","B":"2.0 J","C":"1.0 J","D":"0.50 J"},"correct":"C"},"spec":"PHYS-1","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-phys-3.4"},{"id":"PHYS-N1-16","stem":"The Young modulus of a material is defined as:","options":{"A":"Force × area","B":"Stress ÷ strain","C":"Force ÷ extension","D":"Strain ÷ stress"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GPHYS-SPRINGS-MATERIALS","scaffold":"Young modulus E = stress ÷ strain, where stress = F ÷ A in pascals and strain = ΔL ÷ L and has no units. E therefore has the same units as stress, Pa. It is a property of the material, unlike the spring constant, which also depends on the specimen's dimensions. On a stress–strain graph the gradient of the straight portion gives E, and the area beneath gives energy per unit volume.","reforge":{"stem":"Strain has no units because it is:","options":{"A":"Measured in pascals","B":"Always a very small number","C":"A force per unit area","D":"A ratio of two lengths"},"correct":"D"},"spec":"PHYS-1","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-phys-3.4"},{"id":"PHYS-N1-17","stem":"A material that undergoes very little plastic deformation before breaking is described as:","options":{"A":"Elastic","B":"Ductile","C":"Brittle","D":"Malleable"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GPHYS-SPRINGS-MATERIALS","scaffold":"Brittle materials such as glass and ceramics break soon after the elastic limit with little plastic deformation. Ductile materials such as copper deform plastically a great deal and can be drawn into wires. Elastic behaviour means returning to the original shape when the load is removed; plastic means a permanent change remains. A stress–strain graph distinguishes them by how much curve follows the straight elastic section.","reforge":{"stem":"Plastic deformation means that when the load is removed the material:","options":{"A":"Keeps a permanent change in shape","B":"Becomes stronger than before","C":"Returns exactly to its original length","D":"Breaks immediately"},"correct":"A"},"spec":"PHYS-1","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-phys-3.4"},{"id":"PHYS-N1-18","stem":"A projectile is launched horizontally. Ignoring air resistance, its horizontal velocity:","options":{"A":"Decreases steadily","B":"Increases with time","C":"Reaches zero at the top","D":"Remains constant"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GPHYS-MOTION-GRAVITY","scaffold":"Projectile motion is analysed by treating the horizontal and vertical components independently. Horizontally there is no force (ignoring drag), so velocity is constant. Vertically the acceleration is g downwards, so vertical velocity changes uniformly. The two are linked only by time. This is why a ball dropped and a ball fired horizontally from the same height land together.","reforge":{"stem":"A ball is dropped and another is fired horizontally from the same height at the same moment. They land:","options":{"A":"Depending on the horizontal speed","B":"At the same time","C":"The fired one first","D":"The dropped one first"},"correct":"B"},"spec":"PHYS-1","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-phys-3.5"},{"id":"PHYS-N1-19","stem":"An object moves in a circle at constant speed. Its acceleration is:","options":{"A":"Directed towards the centre","B":"Directed along the velocity","C":"Zero, since the speed is constant","D":"Directed away from the centre"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GPHYS-MOTION-GRAVITY","scaffold":"In uniform circular motion the speed is constant but the velocity is not, because its direction changes continuously. The acceleration, and so the resultant force, points towards the centre — centripetal, meaning centre-seeking. Its magnitude is v²/r or ω²r. There is no outward 'centrifugal force' acting on the body; the outward sensation is the body's inertia.","reforge":{"stem":"The centripetal force on a car cornering on a level road is provided by:","options":{"A":"The weight of the car","B":"The normal contact force","C":"Friction between tyres and road","D":"The engine's forward driving force"},"correct":"C"},"spec":"PHYS-1","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-phys-3.4"},{"id":"PHYS-N1-20","stem":"Newton's law of gravitation states that the force between two masses is proportional to:","options":{"A":"The difference of the masses only","B":"The masses, inversely to separation squared","C":"The sum of the masses and inversely to their separation distance","D":"The sum of the masses and inversely to their separation"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GPHYS-MOTION-GRAVITY","scaffold":"F = Gm₁m₂/r², an inverse square law, always attractive, acting along the line joining the centres of mass. Doubling the separation quarters the force. Gravitational field strength g = F/m in N kg⁻¹, and near a spherical mass g = GM/r². Inside a uniform shell the field is zero. The same inverse square form appears in Coulomb's law for electric charges.","reforge":{"stem":"If the distance between two masses is doubled, the gravitational force between them becomes:","options":{"A":"Half as large","B":"Four times as large","C":"Twice as large","D":"A quarter as large"},"correct":"D"},"spec":"PHYS-1","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-phys-3.4"},{"id":"PHYS-N1-21","stem":"For a satellite to remain above one fixed point on the ground, it must orbit:","options":{"A":"Over the poles at a very low altitude","B":"Over the poles at low altitude","C":"Above the equator, period 24 h","D":"With a period of 12 hours"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GPHYS-ASTRONOMY","scaffold":"A geostationary satellite stays above one point on the Earth's surface, so it must orbit above the equator, in the same direction as the Earth's rotation, with a period of 24 hours. That period fixes the radius through Kepler's third law, T² ∝ r³, giving about 36,000 km altitude. Polar orbits are lower and faster, giving whole-surface coverage over successive passes, which suits imaging rather than communications.","reforge":{"stem":"Kepler's third law relates orbital period T and radius r by:","options":{"A":"T² ∝ r³","B":"T³ ∝ r²","C":"T ∝ r","D":"T ∝ 1/r"},"correct":"A"},"spec":"PHYS-1","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-phys-3.4"},{"id":"PHYS-N1-22","stem":"The unit of the physical quantity found from force × distance is the:","options":{"A":"Watt","B":"Pascal","C":"Newton","D":"Joule"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GPHYS-MEASUREMENT","scaffold":"Force × distance gives work or energy, measured in joules, where 1 J = 1 N m. Base SI units are kg, m, s, A, K, mol and cd; derived units are combinations, so the joule is kg m² s⁻². Checking that both sides of an equation reduce to the same base units — homogeneity — is a quick way to detect an incorrectly recalled formula.","reforge":{"stem":"Expressed in SI base units, the joule is equivalent to:","options":{"A":"kg m s⁻²","B":"kg m² s⁻²","C":"kg m² s⁻³","D":"kg m⁻¹ s⁻²"},"correct":"B"},"spec":"PHYS-1","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-phys-3.4"},{"id":"PHYS-N1-23","stem":"A measurement has a small random error but a large systematic error. The results are:","options":{"A":"Precise but not accurate","B":"Both accurate and precise","C":"Accurate but not precise","D":"Neither accurate nor precise"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GPHYS-MEASUREMENT","scaffold":"Precision describes how closely repeated readings agree and is limited by random error; accuracy describes how close they are to the true value and is spoilt by systematic error. Random errors are reduced by repeating and averaging; systematic errors are not, and must be found and corrected — a zero error on an instrument being the classic example. A tightly grouped but consistently offset set of readings is precise but inaccurate.","reforge":{"stem":"Repeating a measurement and taking a mean reduces:","options":{"A":"Systematic error","B":"Zero error","C":"Random error","D":"Percentage uncertainty in the instrument"},"correct":"C"},"spec":"PHYS-1","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-phys-3.1"},{"id":"PHYS-N1-24","stem":"A length is measured as 25.0 cm with an absolute uncertainty of 0.5 cm. The percentage uncertainty is:","options":{"A":"0.5%","B":"2.0%","C":"12.5%","D":"5.0%"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GPHYS-MEASUREMENT","scaffold":"Percentage uncertainty = (absolute uncertainty ÷ measurement) × 100 = (0.5 ÷ 25.0) × 100 = 2.0%. When quantities are multiplied or divided, add their percentage uncertainties; when added or subtracted, add their absolute uncertainties. When a quantity is raised to a power n, multiply its percentage uncertainty by n — so a squared term doubles it.","reforge":{"stem":"A quantity with 3% uncertainty is cubed. The uncertainty in the result is:","options":{"A":"3%","B":"1%","C":"27%","D":"9%"},"correct":"D"},"spec":"PHYS-1","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-phys-3.1"},{"id":"PHYS-N1-25","stem":"A displacement–time graph is a horizontal straight line. The object is:","options":{"A":"Accelerating uniformly","B":"Moving at constant velocity","C":"Stationary","D":"Decelerating"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GPHYS-GRAPHS-KINEMATICS","scaffold":"A horizontal line on a displacement–time graph has zero gradient, so velocity is zero and the object is stationary. A straight sloping line means constant velocity, and a curve means changing velocity, so acceleration. On a velocity–time graph a horizontal line means constant velocity, and a horizontal line at zero means stationary — the same shape carries different meaning depending on the axes.","reforge":{"stem":"A velocity–time graph is a horizontal line above the time axis. The object is:","options":{"A":"Moving at constant velocity","B":"Changing direction continuously","C":"Stationary","D":"Changing direction"},"correct":"A"},"spec":"PHYS-1","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-phys-3.4"},{"id":"PHYS-N1-26","stem":"The terminal velocity of a falling object is reached when:","options":{"A":"Its weight exceeds the drag force","B":"Air resistance becomes zero","C":"Its acceleration reaches g","D":"The drag force equals its weight"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GPHYS-RESULTANT-FORCE","scaffold":"As speed increases, drag increases until it balances weight; the resultant force is then zero, acceleration is zero and the object falls at constant terminal velocity. Note it is still moving, just no longer speeding up. On a velocity–time graph the curve flattens to a horizontal line. Increasing the surface area, as with a parachute, raises drag at a given speed and so lowers terminal velocity.","reforge":{"stem":"A skydiver opens a parachute. Their terminal velocity:","options":{"A":"Increases, since the drag force rises","B":"Decreases, since drag rises","C":"Falls to zero immediately","D":"Stays the same"},"correct":"B"},"spec":"PHYS-1","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-phys-3.4"},{"id":"PHYS-N1-27","stem":"A 60 W lamp is switched on for 300 s. The energy transferred is:","options":{"A":"18000 J","B":"5 J","C":"360 joules","D":"360 J"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GPHYS-WORK-ENERGY","scaffold":"Energy = power × time = 60 × 300 = 18,000 J, or 18 kJ. Watts are joules per second, so the time must be in seconds — using minutes here would give an answer 60 times too small. In electrical contexts the same relationship appears as E = VIt, since power P = VI, and the kilowatt-hour used for domestic billing is 3.6 × 10⁶ J.","reforge":{"stem":"Power is related to force and velocity for steady motion by:","options":{"A":"P = F + v","B":"P = F ÷ v","C":"P = Fv","D":"P = Fv²"},"correct":"C"},"spec":"PHYS-1","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-phys-3.4"},{"id":"PHYS-N1-28","stem":"A car of mass 1200 kg accelerates from 10 m s⁻¹ to 20 m s⁻¹. The increase in kinetic energy is:","options":{"A":"60 kJ","B":"180 kJ","C":"120 kJ","D":"240 kJ"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GPHYS-WORK-ENERGY","scaffold":"Kinetic energy before = ½ × 1200 × 100 = 60,000 J; after = ½ × 1200 × 400 = 240,000 J. The increase is 180,000 J = 180 kJ. Because v is squared, doubling the speed quadruples the kinetic energy, so the increase is three times the original value — not double. Calculating ½m(v − u)² instead of the difference of the two energies is the standard error.","reforge":{"stem":"Doubling an object's speed changes its kinetic energy by a factor of:","options":{"A":"2","B":"8","C":"√2","D":"4"},"correct":"D"},"spec":"PHYS-1","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-phys-3.4"},{"id":"PHYS-N1-29","stem":"A force of 5.0 N acts for 4.0 s on a body. The impulse delivered is:","options":{"A":"0.8 N s","B":"1.25 N s","C":"20 N s","D":"9.0 N s"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GPHYS-MOTION-GRAVITY","scaffold":"Impulse = FΔt = 5.0 × 4.0 = 20 N s, and equals the change in momentum, so its unit is equivalently kg m s⁻¹. On a force–time graph the area beneath gives the impulse. Extending the time over which momentum changes reduces the force required — the principle behind crumple zones, airbags and bending the knees on landing.","reforge":{"stem":"A car's crumple zone reduces the force on occupants in a crash by:","options":{"A":"Increasing the time taken to stop","B":"Increasing the mass of the car","C":"Reducing the change in momentum","D":"Reducing the total change in momentum"},"correct":"A"},"spec":"PHYS-1","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-phys-3.4"},{"id":"PHYS-N1-30","stem":"Two forces act on an object in opposite directions along the same line: 15 N and 9 N. The resultant is:","options":{"A":"24 N","B":"1.67 N","C":"135 N","D":"6 N"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GPHYS-RESULTANT-FORCE","scaffold":"Antiparallel forces subtract: 15 − 9 = 6 N, acting in the direction of the larger force. Parallel forces in the same direction add to 24 N. Perpendicular forces combine by Pythagoras. For forces at other angles, resolve each into horizontal and vertical components, sum each direction separately, then recombine — the general method that covers all these special cases.","reforge":{"stem":"To combine forces acting at an arbitrary angle, the general method is to:","options":{"A":"Add their magnitudes directly","B":"Resolve, then recombine","C":"Add their magnitudes together directly","D":"Multiply the magnitudes together"},"correct":"B"},"spec":"PHYS-1","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-phys-3.4"},{"id":"PHYS-N1-31","stem":"A body of mass 0.5 kg has momentum 4.0 kg m s⁻¹. Its velocity is:","options":{"A":"8.0 m s⁻¹","B":"0.125 m s⁻¹","C":"2.0 m s⁻¹","D":"4.5 m s⁻¹"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GPHYS-MOTION-GRAVITY","scaffold":"Momentum p = mv, so v = p ÷ m = 4.0 ÷ 0.5 = 8.0 m s⁻¹. Dividing the wrong way round gives 0.125. Momentum is a vector in the direction of the velocity, measured in kg m s⁻¹ or equivalently N s. Newton's second law in its general form states that resultant force equals the rate of change of momentum, which reduces to F = ma when mass is constant.","reforge":{"stem":"Newton's second law in its general form states that resultant force equals the:","options":{"A":"Change in kinetic energy","B":"Product of mass and velocity","C":"Rate of change of momentum","D":"Rate of change of displacement"},"correct":"C"},"spec":"PHYS-1","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-phys-3.4"},{"id":"PHYS-N1-32","stem":"A trolley is released on a frictionless slope. As it descends, its gravitational potential energy:","options":{"A":"Increases as kinetic energy increases","B":"Converts to kinetic energy","C":"Is destroyed","D":"Increases as the kinetic energy also increases"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GPHYS-WORK-ENERGY","scaffold":"With no resistive forces, mechanical energy is conserved: the loss in gravitational potential energy equals the gain in kinetic energy, so mgΔh = ½mv². The mass cancels, which is why the speed at the bottom of a frictionless slope does not depend on mass. When friction or drag acts, some energy becomes internal energy and the final kinetic energy is correspondingly smaller.","reforge":{"stem":"An object falls 5.0 m from rest with no air resistance. Using g = 9.81 m s⁻², its speed on landing is:","options":{"A":"49 m s⁻¹","B":"5.0 m s⁻¹","C":"25 m s⁻¹","D":"9.9 m s⁻¹"},"correct":"D"},"spec":"PHYS-1","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-phys-3.4"},{"id":"PHYS-N1-33","stem":"The centre of mass of a uniform rectangular sheet is located:","options":{"A":"At one corner","B":"Along its longest edge","C":"At its centre","D":"Outside the sheet"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GPHYS-RESULTANT-FORCE","scaffold":"For a uniform object the centre of mass sits at the geometric centre. It is the point where the whole weight can be taken to act, which simplifies moment calculations. For an irregular shape it can be found by suspending the object from two different points and marking the vertical through each. An object topples when the vertical line through its centre of mass falls outside its base.","reforge":{"stem":"An object topples over when the vertical line through its centre of mass:","options":{"A":"Falls outside the base","B":"Is parallel to the base","C":"Passes through the centre of the base","D":"Passes through the highest point"},"correct":"A"},"spec":"PHYS-1","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-phys-3.4"},{"id":"PHYS-N1-34","stem":"An object decelerates uniformly from 20 m s⁻¹ to rest in 5.0 s. Its acceleration is:","options":{"A":"4.0 m s⁻²","B":"−0.25 m s⁻²","C":"−100 m s⁻²","D":"−4.0 m s⁻²"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GPHYS-GRAPHS-KINEMATICS","scaffold":"Acceleration = (v − u) ÷ t = (0 − 20) ÷ 5.0 = −4.0 m s⁻². The negative sign shows the acceleration opposes the motion, which is what deceleration means; quoting the magnitude alone loses the direction information. Sign conventions matter throughout kinematics: choose a positive direction at the start and apply it consistently to displacement, velocity and acceleration.","reforge":{"stem":"Using v² = u² + 2as, an object decelerating at 4.0 m s⁻² from 20 m s⁻¹ stops after:","options":{"A":"5.0 m","B":"50 m","C":"25 m","D":"100 m"},"correct":"B"},"spec":"PHYS-1","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-phys-3.4"},{"id":"PHYS-N1-35","stem":"Which pair correctly matches a quantity with its SI unit?","options":{"A":"Momentum — kg m s⁻¹","B":"Pressure — measured in newtons","C":"Force — pascal","D":"Power — joule"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GPHYS-MEASUREMENT","scaffold":"Momentum is kg m s⁻¹, equivalently N s. Force is measured in newtons, pressure in pascals (N m⁻²), energy in joules and power in watts (J s⁻¹). Confusing energy with power is the commonest slip: energy is a total, power is a rate. Checking units is a fast way to test whether a recalled formula is right, since an equation must be dimensionally consistent.","reforge":{"stem":"The pascal is equivalent to:","options":{"A":"J s⁻¹","B":"N m","C":"N m⁻²","D":"kg m s⁻²"},"correct":"C"},"spec":"PHYS-1","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-phys-3.4"},{"id":"PHYS-N1-36","stem":"A stress–strain graph for a ductile metal shows a straight line followed by a curve. The straight portion represents:","options":{"A":"Plastic deformation","B":"Elastic, obeying Hooke","C":"The breaking stress","D":"Permanent plastic deformation"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GPHYS-SPRINGS-MATERIALS","scaffold":"The straight section shows proportional, elastic behaviour, with gradient equal to the Young modulus. The limit of proportionality is where the line first curves; the elastic limit follows, beyond which deformation is permanent. The yield point is where extension increases sharply for little extra stress, and the ultimate tensile stress is the maximum before fracture.","reforge":{"stem":"The gradient of the straight section of a stress–strain graph gives the:","options":{"A":"Spring constant","B":"Strain energy","C":"Breaking stress","D":"Young modulus"},"correct":"D"},"spec":"PHYS-1","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-phys-3.4"},{"id":"PHYS-N1-37","stem":"A wire of cross-sectional area 2.0 × 10⁻⁶ m² carries a tension of 40 N. The stress is:","options":{"A":"5.0 × 10⁻⁸ Pa","B":"8.0 × 10⁻⁵ Pa","C":"2.0 × 10⁷ Pa","D":"40 Pa"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GPHYS-SPRINGS-MATERIALS","scaffold":"Stress = force ÷ area = 40 ÷ (2.0 × 10⁻⁶) = 2.0 × 10⁷ Pa. Dividing by a small power of ten produces a large answer, so a result smaller than the force signals an inverted calculation. If the diameter is given rather than the area, find A = πr² first, remembering to halve the diameter — forgetting that step introduces a factor of four.","reforge":{"stem":"A wire of original length 2.0 m extends by 4.0 mm. The strain is:","options":{"A":"2.0 × 10⁻³","B":"8.0 × 10⁻³","C":"0.50","D":"2.0"},"correct":"A"},"spec":"PHYS-1","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-phys-3.4"},{"id":"PHYS-N1-38","stem":"Two objects of different mass are dropped in a vacuum. They:","options":{"A":"Fall with acceleration proportional to mass","B":"Fall at constant velocity","C":"Reach different terminal velocities","D":"Fall with the same acceleration"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GPHYS-MOTION-GRAVITY","scaffold":"In a vacuum the only force is weight, W = mg, so acceleration a = F ÷ m = mg ÷ m = g for every mass. The mass cancels, so all objects fall at the same rate. In air, drag depends on shape and area rather than mass alone, which is why a feather falls more slowly — the difference is due to air resistance, not to gravity acting differently.","reforge":{"stem":"A feather falls more slowly than a stone in air because of the difference in:","options":{"A":"Their masses being different","B":"Air resistance, not gravity","C":"Their masses only","D":"Weight alone"},"correct":"B"},"spec":"PHYS-1","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-phys-3.4"},{"id":"PHYS-N1-39","stem":"The gravitational field strength at a point is defined as:","options":{"A":"Force per unit mass","B":"Energy stored per unit mass","C":"Energy per unit mass","D":"Mass per unit volume"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GPHYS-MOTION-GRAVITY","scaffold":"Gravitational field strength g = F ÷ m, measured in N kg⁻¹, and is numerically equal to the free-fall acceleration in m s⁻². Around a point or spherical mass, g = GM/r², falling with the inverse square of distance from the centre. Field lines point towards the mass and their spacing indicates field strength; uniformly spaced parallel lines represent a uniform field, as near the Earth's surface.","reforge":{"stem":"Gravitational field lines near the Earth's surface are drawn parallel and evenly spaced because the field there is:","options":{"A":"Radial and steadily weakening","B":"Zero","C":"Uniform over small distances","D":"Repulsive"},"correct":"C"},"spec":"PHYS-1","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-phys-3.7"},{"id":"PHYS-N1-40","stem":"An astronaut in orbit appears weightless because they are:","options":{"A":"Beyond the Earth's gravitational field entirely","B":"In free fall together with the spacecraft","C":"Moving too fast for gravity to act","D":"Shielded by the spacecraft"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GPHYS-ASTRONOMY","scaffold":"In orbit gravity is still strong — it provides the centripetal force keeping the spacecraft in its path. Both astronaut and craft accelerate towards the Earth at the same rate, so there is no contact force between them and no sensation of weight. Apparent weightlessness therefore means free fall, not the absence of gravity, which is the most common misconception in this topic.","reforge":{"stem":"The centripetal force keeping a satellite in orbit is provided by:","options":{"A":"Its engines firing continuously","B":"The satellite's own momentum","C":"Air resistance in the upper atmosphere","D":"Gravitational attraction to the Earth"},"correct":"D"},"spec":"PHYS-1","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-phys-3.4"},{"id":"PHYS-N1-41","stem":"A student records 5 repeat readings that differ slightly. The best estimate of the true value is:","options":{"A":"The most recent reading taken","B":"The smallest reading","C":"The mean of the readings","D":"The largest reading"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GPHYS-MEASUREMENT","scaffold":"Averaging repeats reduces the effect of random error, so the mean is the best estimate. Anomalous results should be identified and excluded before averaging, with a reason given. The uncertainty may be estimated as half the range of the readings. Repeating does nothing for systematic error, which shifts every reading in the same direction and must be traced to its cause.","reforge":{"stem":"Readings of 4.1, 4.2, 4.0 and 6.8 are recorded. The correct treatment is to:","options":{"A":"Exclude 6.8, giving a reason","B":"Use only the largest value","C":"Average all four values","D":"Repeat until 6.8 appears again"},"correct":"A"},"spec":"PHYS-1","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-phys-3.1"},{"id":"PHYS-N1-42","stem":"A quantity is stated as 4.50 × 10³ m. The number of significant figures is:","options":{"A":"Two","B":"Six","C":"Four","D":"3"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GPHYS-MEASUREMENT","scaffold":"In standard form only the coefficient carries significant figures, so 4.50 × 10³ has three — the trailing zero counts because it is stated deliberately. A calculated result should be quoted to the same number of significant figures as the least precise input, and intermediate values should be carried at full precision to avoid rounding errors accumulating.","reforge":{"stem":"A calculation uses values given to 2 and 4 significant figures. The answer should be quoted to:","options":{"A":"3 significant figures","B":"2 significant figures","C":"6 significant figures","D":"4 significant figures"},"correct":"B"},"spec":"PHYS-1","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-phys-3.1"},{"id":"PHYS-N1-43","stem":"A velocity–time graph shows a straight line sloping downwards to cross the time axis. After crossing, the object is:","options":{"A":"Moving the opposite way","B":"Accelerating forwards","C":"Stationary","D":"Moving at a constant speed"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GPHYS-GRAPHS-KINEMATICS","scaffold":"Below the time axis the velocity is negative, so the object moves in the opposite direction to before. The gradient is unchanged, so the acceleration is constant throughout — the object slowed, stopped momentarily at the crossing point, and then reversed. Area below the axis counts as negative displacement, so the total displacement may be less than the total distance travelled.","reforge":{"stem":"On a velocity–time graph, area below the time axis represents:","options":{"A":"Zero displacement","B":"Negative acceleration","C":"Negative displacement","D":"Distance travelled forwards only"},"correct":"C"},"spec":"PHYS-1","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-phys-3.4"},{"id":"PHYS-N1-44","stem":"A lift accelerates upwards. The reading on a scale beneath a passenger is:","options":{"A":"Zero","B":"Greater than their true weight","C":"Equal to their true weight throughout","D":"Equal to their true weight"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GPHYS-RESULTANT-FORCE","scaffold":"The scale reads the normal contact force. Accelerating upwards requires a net upward force, so N − W = ma and N = m(g + a), greater than the weight. Accelerating downwards gives N = m(g − a), less than the weight, and in free fall a = g so N = 0, which is apparent weightlessness. The true weight mg is unchanged throughout; only the contact force varies.","reforge":{"stem":"A lift's cable breaks and it falls freely. The scale reading beneath a passenger is:","options":{"A":"Double their weight","B":"Half their weight","C":"Their true weight","D":"Zero"},"correct":"D"},"spec":"PHYS-1","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-phys-3.4"},{"id":"PHYS-N1-45","stem":"An object is dropped from a height and air resistance is significant. As it falls, its acceleration:","options":{"A":"Stays constant at g","B":"Increases steadily","C":"Decreases towards zero","D":"Becomes negative immediately"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GPHYS-RESULTANT-FORCE","scaffold":"At release, drag is zero and acceleration equals g. As speed rises, drag rises, so the resultant force and therefore acceleration fall. When drag equals weight the acceleration reaches zero and the object continues at terminal velocity. Note the object keeps speeding up throughout — it is the rate of increase that falls, which is why the velocity–time curve flattens rather than turning downwards.","reforge":{"stem":"While an object approaches terminal velocity, its speed is:","options":{"A":"Still increasing, but more slowly","B":"Constant from the moment of release","C":"Decreasing","D":"Zero"},"correct":"A"},"spec":"PHYS-1","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-phys-3.5"}]},"PHYS-2":{"label":"Mechanics, Electricity & Waves","color":"#4f46e5","questions":[{"id":"PHYS-09","stem":"A ball is thrown vertically upward. At the highest point of its flight, what are its velocity and acceleration?","options":{"A":"Velocity zero, acceleration 9.81 m s⁻² downward","B":"Velocity zero, acceleration zero — the ball is momentarily at rest.","C":"Velocity 9.81 m s⁻¹ upward, acceleration zero.","D":"Both velocity and acceleration point upward."},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GPHYS-MOTION-GRAVITY","scaffold":"At the apex, velocity = 0 (the ball changes direction) but acceleration = g = 9.81 m s⁻² downward throughout the flight (only gravity acts, ignoring air resistance). Common error: assuming a = 0 because v = 0. Velocity and acceleration are independent — an object can have zero velocity while accelerating. The ball decelerates going up, stops, then accelerates down, all under constant g. This is the classic misconception that 'no motion means no force'.","reforge":{"stem":"A car momentarily at rest at the top of a hill, about to roll back down, has:","options":{"A":"Zero velocity and zero acceleration.","B":"Maximum velocity and zero acceleration.","C":"Zero velocity, non-zero acceleration","D":"Zero acceleration because it is not yet moving."},"correct":"C"},"spec":"PHYS-2","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-phys-3.4"},{"id":"PHYS-10","stem":"Two resistors, 4 Ω and 12 Ω, are connected in PARALLEL. What is their combined resistance?","options":{"A":"The force that pushes electrons, measured in newtons.","B":"3 Ω — using 1/R = 1/4 + 1/12 = 4/12, so R = 3 Ω","C":"8 Ω — the average of the two.","D":"48 Ω — multiply the two values."},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GPHYS-CIRCUITS","scaffold":"Series: R = R1 + R2 (resistances add). Parallel: 1/R = 1/R1 + 1/R2, and the total is ALWAYS smaller than the smallest individual resistor (more paths for current). Here 1/R = 1/4 + 1/12 = 3/12 + 1/12 = 4/12, R = 3 Ω. Sense check: 3 Ω < 4 Ω ✓. Common errors: adding parallel resistances directly, or forgetting to invert 1/R at the end. For two resistors only, the product-over-sum shortcut works: (4×12)/(4+12) = 48/16 = 3 Ω.","reforge":{"stem":"The same two resistors (4 Ω and 12 Ω) are now connected in SERIES. The combined resistance is:","options":{"A":"The current the battery supplies, measured in amps.","B":"3 Ω, as in the parallel case.","C":"8 Ω, the average.","D":"16 Ω — series resistances add directly: 4 + 12."},"correct":"D"},"spec":"PHYS-2","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-phys-3.5"},{"id":"PHYS-11","stem":"A student states that in a transverse wave 'the particles move in the same direction as the wave travels'. Correct this.","options":{"A":"Transverse waves travel through space but cannot transfer energy to matter.","B":"Particles remain completely stationary in all wave types — only energy moves.","C":"In a transverse wave, particles oscillate perpendicular to the wave's direction of travel","D":"A node is a point of maximum displacement where the two waves always constructively interfere."},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GPHYS-WAVES","scaffold":"Transverse: particle oscillation perpendicular to energy transfer (e.g. light, EM waves, water surface, waves on a string). Longitudinal: particle oscillation parallel to energy transfer, with compressions and rarefactions (e.g. sound). In BOTH, particles oscillate about a fixed point — they do NOT travel with the wave; only energy propagates. Only transverse waves can be polarised. Common error: thinking matter moves along with the wave.","reforge":{"stem":"Which property is unique to transverse waves and provides evidence for their nature?","options":{"A":"They can be polarised","B":"They can reflect off surfaces.","C":"They can be refracted.","D":"They transfer energy without transferring matter."},"correct":"A"},"spec":"PHYS-2","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-phys-3.3"},{"id":"PHYS-12","stem":"A 2 kg object experiences a resultant force of 10 N. A student calculates its acceleration as 20 m s⁻² (F × m). What is the correct value?","options":{"A":"20 m s⁻² — the student multiplied force by mass correctly.","B":"0.2 m s⁻² — calculated as m ÷ F rather than F ÷ m.","C":"12 m s⁻² — calculated as F + m rather than F ÷ m.","D":"5 m s⁻² — F = ma rearranges to a = F/m = 10 ÷ 2 = 5 m s⁻²."},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GPHYS-RESULTANT-FORCE","scaffold":"Newton's second law: F = ma, so a = F/m (force divided by mass, not multiplied). Here a = 10/2 = 5 m s⁻². Resultant (net) force matters — sum all forces first, accounting for direction. Units: N = kg m s⁻². Common errors: multiplying instead of dividing, using weight instead of mass, or using a single force instead of the resultant. Always identify the resultant force before applying F = ma.","reforge":{"stem":"The same 2 kg object is now pushed with 10 N forward while a 4 N friction force opposes it. Its acceleration is:","options":{"A":"Scalars and vectors are interchangeable — the distinction doesn't matter in calculations.","B":"3 m s⁻² — resultant force = 10 − 4 = 6 N, so a = 6/2 = 3 m s⁻².","C":"5 m s⁻² — ignoring friction.","D":"2 m s⁻² — a = friction/mass."},"correct":"B"},"spec":"PHYS-2","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-phys-3.4"},{"id":"PHYS-13","stem":"Distinguish clearly between scalar and vector quantities. Which set contains ONLY vectors?","options":{"A":"Displacement, velocity, acceleration, force — all have magnitude AND direction.","B":"Speed, distance, energy, temperature — all magnitude only.","C":"Mass, time, speed, power — all scalar quantities with magnitude only.","D":"Distance, speed, mass, work — scalars that are sometimes confused with vectors."},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GPHYS-SCALAR-VECTOR","scaffold":"Vectors (magnitude AND direction): displacement, velocity, acceleration, force, momentum, weight. Scalars (magnitude only): distance, speed, mass, time, energy, temperature, work, power. Watch the pairs: distance (scalar) vs displacement (vector); speed (scalar) vs velocity (vector). Weight is a vector (force); mass is a scalar. Vectors add using tip-to-tail/pythagoras; scalars add arithmetically. Common trap: classifying work or energy as vectors — they are scalars despite involving force.","reforge":{"stem":"A runner completes one lap of a 400 m track, finishing where they started. Their distance and displacement are:","options":{"A":"Distance 400 m, displacement 400 m.","B":"Distance 0 m, displacement 400 m.","C":"Distance 400 m, displacement 0 m","D":"Both are zero."},"correct":"C"},"spec":"PHYS-2","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-phys-3.5"},{"id":"PHYS-14","stem":"In a simple circuit, what does the electromotive force (e.m.f.) of a battery actually represent?","options":{"A":"The student is correct — current does decrease around a series circuit.","B":"The energy transferred to each coulomb of charge by the battery","C":"The resistance inside the battery.","D":"The current the battery supplies, measured in amps."},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GPHYS-CIRCUITS","scaffold":"e.m.f. = energy transferred TO charge per coulomb by the source (J/C = V). Potential difference = energy transferred FROM charge per coulomb to a component. Despite its name, e.m.f. is NOT a force — it is an energy-per-charge quantity in volts. Terminal p.d. < e.m.f. because of 'lost volts' across internal resistance r: e.m.f. = I(R + r). Common errors: calling e.m.f. a force, or confusing it with current. V = W/Q underlies both e.m.f. and p.d.","reforge":{"stem":"A battery has e.m.f. 12 V but its terminal potential difference is only 11.5 V when supplying current. Why?","options":{"A":"The battery is faulty and should read 12 V.","B":"e.m.f. always exceeds terminal p.d. by exactly 0.5 V.","C":"The voltmeter is measuring current instead.","D":"Some energy per coulomb is lost inside the cell"},"correct":"D"},"spec":"PHYS-2","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-phys-3.4"},{"id":"PHYS-15","stem":"A student says heavier objects fall faster in a vacuum. Referring to free fall, why is this wrong?","options":{"A":"An external gravitational field acting on it under standard conditions in a typical experiment","B":"Lighter objects fall faster in a vacuum because they have less resistance.","C":"In a vacuum all objects fall with the same acceleration g — mass cancels in a = F/m = mg/m.","D":"Objects do not fall in a vacuum as there is no air to push them down."},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GPHYS-MOTION-GRAVITY","scaffold":"In free fall (vacuum): a = g for all masses. Reasoning via F = ma: gravitational force W = mg, so a = W/m = mg/m = g — mass cancels. A heavier object feels a bigger force but needs a bigger force to accelerate equally (more inertia). In air, resistance depends on shape/speed, so a feather falls slower — but that is air resistance, not gravity. The famous Apollo 15 hammer-feather drop demonstrated equal fall in a vacuum.","reforge":{"stem":"In air, a feather falls more slowly than a coin. In the mechanics of this, the key factor is:","options":{"A":"Air resistance affects the feather far more","B":"The coin experiences a stronger gravitational field.","C":"The feather has more inertia than the coin.","D":"Gravity does not act on feathers."},"correct":"A"},"spec":"PHYS-2","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-phys-3.5"},{"id":"PHYS-16","stem":"A 500 g mass hangs from a spring, extending it by 0.10 m. Taking g = 9.81 m s⁻², what is the spring constant k?","options":{"A":"To ensure the structure carries no load at all under standard test conditions in a typical experiment","B":"5000 N m⁻¹ — using the mass in grams.","C":"0.98 N m⁻¹ — dividing extension by force.","D":"49.05 N m⁻¹ — using F = kx with F = mg = 0.500 × 9.81 = 4.905 N, so k = 4.905/0.10"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GPHYS-SPRINGS-MATERIALS","scaffold":"Hooke's law: F = kx, so k = F/x. The force is the WEIGHT: F = mg = 0.500 kg × 9.81 = 4.905 N (convert 500 g to 0.500 kg). Then k = 4.905/0.10 = 49.05 N m⁻¹. Common errors: using mass in grams, using mass instead of weight for F, or dividing x by F. Elastic potential energy stored = ½kx² = ½Fx (area under a force-extension graph). Units of k: N m⁻¹.","reforge":{"stem":"How much elastic potential energy is stored in the spring at this 0.10 m extension?","options":{"A":"The spring has already passed its elastic limit under standard conditions","B":"0.49 J — using E = ½kx² = ½ × 49.05 × 0.10².","C":"0.245 J — using ½mx.","D":"49.05 J — using kx."},"correct":"B"},"spec":"PHYS-2","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-phys-3.5"},{"id":"PHYS-E2-01","spec":"PHYS-2","stem":"The electric field strength at a point is defined as:","options":{"A":"force per unit positive charge","B":"energy per unit mass","C":"charge per unit time","D":"potential difference per current"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GPHYS-FIELDS-ELECTROMAGNETISM","scaffold":"E = F/Q and field direction is the force direction on a positive test charge.","reforge":{"stem":"A small positive test charge is placed near a charged sphere, and the force on it is measured. Dividing that force by the size of the test charge gives what quantity?","options":{"A":"The electric field strength at that point","B":"The energy stored per unit mass","C":"The charge flowing per unit time","D":"The potential difference per unit current"},"correct":"A"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-phys-3.7"},{"id":"PHYS-E2-02","spec":"PHYS-2","stem":"The magnetic force on a current-carrying wire is greatest when the wire is:","options":{"A":"The potential difference per unit current","B":"perpendicular to the magnetic field","C":"outside every field","D":"at zero current"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GPHYS-FIELDS-ELECTROMAGNETISM","scaffold":"The force is BIL sin θ and is maximum when θ is 90 degrees.","reforge":{"stem":"A current-carrying wire is rotated within a fixed magnetic field, and the force on it changes as the angle changes. At which orientation is this force at its maximum?","options":{"A":"When the wire is parallel to the magnetic field","B":"When the wire is perpendicular to the magnetic field","C":"When the wire is placed outside the field entirely under standard conditions","D":"When there is zero current in the wire"},"correct":"B"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-phys-3.5"},{"id":"PHYS-E2-03","spec":"PHYS-2","stem":"In a transformer, increasing the number of secondary turns generally:","options":{"A":"reduces the primary frequency","B":"removes all energy losses","C":"increases the secondary potential difference","D":"changes alternating current to direct current"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GPHYS-FIELDS-ELECTROMAGNETISM","scaffold":"For an ideal transformer, Vp/Vs = Np/Ns.","reforge":{"stem":"A step-up transformer is built with far more turns on its secondary coil than its primary coil. What effect does this have on the output?","options":{"A":"It reduces the frequency supplied to the primary coil","B":"It removes all energy losses in the transformer","C":"It increases the secondary potential difference","D":"It converts the alternating current into direct current"},"correct":"C"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-phys-3.5"},{"id":"PHYS-E2-04","spec":"PHYS-2","stem":"Simple harmonic motion has acceleration that is:","options":{"A":"An external gravitational field acting on it under standard conditions in a typical experiment","B":"zero at every point","C":"proportional to velocity only","D":"proportional to displacement and directed towards equilibrium"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GPHYS-OSCILLATIONS-THERMAL","scaffold":"The restoring acceleration is a = −ω²x.","reforge":{"stem":"A mass on a spring oscillates back and forth, always accelerating back towards its central resting position, more strongly the further it is displaced. What describes this acceleration?","options":{"A":"It is constant and always directed away from equilibrium under standard conditions","B":"It is zero at every point in the oscillation","C":"It is proportional only to the object's velocity","D":"It is proportional to displacement and directed towards equilibrium"},"correct":"D"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-phys-3.6"},{"id":"PHYS-E2-05","spec":"PHYS-2","stem":"The internal energy of a system is the sum of its particles':","options":{"A":"random kinetic and potential energies","B":"rest masses only","C":"electric charges only","D":"It is zero at every point in the oscillation"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GPHYS-OSCILLATIONS-THERMAL","scaffold":"Heating or work can transfer energy into or out of internal energy.","reforge":{"stem":"A gas is heated, and its particles move faster and spread further apart on average. What quantity increases as a result?","options":{"A":"Its internal energy, the sum of particles' random kinetic and potential energies","B":"The rest mass of its particles only","C":"The electric charge of its particles only","D":"An external gravitational field acting on it under standard conditions in a typical experiment"},"correct":"A"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-phys-3.6"},{"id":"PHYS-E2-06","spec":"PHYS-2","stem":"A capacitor's time constant is:","options":{"A":"resistance divided by charge","B":"resistance multiplied by capacitance","C":"voltage multiplied by current","D":"Velocity zero, acceleration zero — the ball is momentarily at rest."},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GPHYS-GRAPHS-KINEMATICS","scaffold":"The time constant RC sets the characteristic charging or discharging timescale.","reforge":{"stem":"A capacitor discharges through a resistor, and the time it takes for the charge to fall to about 37% of its initial value depends on two component values. What is this characteristic time called, and how is it found?","options":{"A":"The time constant, found by dividing resistance by charge","B":"The time constant, found by multiplying resistance and capacitance","C":"The time constant, found by multiplying voltage and current","D":"The time constant, found by dividing capacitance by frequency under standard conditions"},"correct":"B"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-phys-3.5"},{"id":"PHYS-N2-01","stem":"Electric current is defined as the rate of flow of:","options":{"A":"Energy","B":"Voltage","C":"Charge","D":"Resistance"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GPHYS-CIRCUITS","scaffold":"Current I = ΔQ/Δt, measured in amperes, where one ampere is one coulomb per second. Conventional current is drawn from positive to negative, whereas electrons actually drift from negative to positive — the convention predates the discovery of the electron. Charge is quantised in units of e = 1.60 × 10⁻¹⁹ C, so a total charge is always a whole number multiple of that value.","reforge":{"stem":"A current of 2.0 A flows for 30 s. The charge transferred is:","options":{"A":"60 C","B":"0.067 C","C":"32 C","D":"15 C"},"correct":"A"},"spec":"PHYS-2","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-phys-3.5"},{"id":"PHYS-N2-02","stem":"Potential difference across a component is defined as:","options":{"A":"Current flowing per unit resistance","B":"Charge per unit time","C":"Force per unit charge","D":"Energy transferred per unit charge"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GPHYS-CIRCUITS","scaffold":"Potential difference V = W/Q, energy transferred per coulomb, measured in volts. Electromotive force is defined the same way but refers to energy supplied per coulomb by a source rather than delivered to a component. The distinction matters in circuits with internal resistance, where terminal pd is less than emf because some energy is dissipated inside the source.","reforge":{"stem":"A cell of emf 12 V has internal resistance. When current flows, the terminal pd is:","options":{"A":"Exactly 12 V","B":"Less than 12 V","C":"Greater than 12 V","D":"Zero"},"correct":"B"},"spec":"PHYS-2","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-phys-3.5"},{"id":"PHYS-N2-03","stem":"A resistor carries 0.50 A with 6.0 V across it. Its resistance is:","options":{"A":"12 Ω","B":"0.083 Ω","C":"6.5 Ω","D":"3.0 Ω"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GPHYS-CIRCUITS","scaffold":"Ohm's law gives R = V ÷ I = 6.0 ÷ 0.50 = 12 Ω. Dividing the wrong way round gives 0.083. Ohm's law applies to an ohmic conductor at constant temperature, where a current–voltage graph is a straight line through the origin. A filament lamp is non-ohmic: as current rises the filament heats, resistance increases and the graph curves.","reforge":{"stem":"The I–V graph for a filament lamp curves rather than being straight because as current increases the filament:","options":{"A":"Cools, lowering resistance","B":"Carries no current","C":"Heats, raising resistance","D":"Becomes superconducting"},"correct":"C"},"spec":"PHYS-2","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-phys-3.5"},{"id":"PHYS-N2-04","stem":"Two 6 Ω resistors are connected in series. The total resistance is:","options":{"A":"3 Ω","B":"12 Ω","C":"36 Ω","D":"0.33 Ω"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GPHYS-CIRCUITS","scaffold":"In series resistances add: R = R₁ + R₂ = 12 Ω, and the total is always larger than the largest individual resistor. In parallel 1/R = 1/R₁ + 1/R₂, so two 6 Ω resistors give 3 Ω, and the total is always smaller than the smallest individual one. Checking the answer against those two rules catches most arithmetic slips immediately.","reforge":{"stem":"Two 6 Ω resistors are connected in parallel. The total resistance is:","options":{"A":"12 Ω","B":"6 Ω","C":"0.33 Ω","D":"3 Ω"},"correct":"D"},"spec":"PHYS-2","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-phys-3.5"},{"id":"PHYS-N2-05","stem":"In a series circuit, the current through each component is:","options":{"A":"Divided between components","B":"Greatest at the battery","C":"The same everywhere","D":"Zero in the last component"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GPHYS-CIRCUITS","scaffold":"In series there is only one path, so current is identical at every point, while the pd divides between components in proportion to their resistance. In parallel each branch has the full supply pd across it, and the currents in the branches add to the total. These follow from conservation of charge at a junction and conservation of energy around a loop — Kirchhoff's two laws.","reforge":{"stem":"Kirchhoff's first law, that currents into a junction equal currents out, follows from conservation of:","options":{"A":"Charge","B":"Momentum","C":"Mass","D":"Energy"},"correct":"A"},"spec":"PHYS-2","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-phys-3.5"},{"id":"PHYS-N2-06","stem":"The power dissipated by a resistor can be calculated as:","options":{"A":"P = V ÷ I","B":"P = IR²","C":"P = V ÷ R","D":"P = I²R"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GPHYS-CIRCUITS","scaffold":"Power P = VI, and substituting Ohm's law gives the equivalent forms P = I²R and P = V²/R. Choose whichever form matches the quantities given. Because power depends on the square of the current, doubling the current quadruples the heating — the reason electrical transmission uses high voltage and low current to reduce losses in the cables.","reforge":{"stem":"Electricity is transmitted at high voltage and low current mainly to reduce:","options":{"A":"The cost of the cables","B":"Power lost as heat in the cables","C":"The voltage delivered to the consumer","D":"The frequency of the supply"},"correct":"B"},"spec":"PHYS-2","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-phys-3.4"},{"id":"PHYS-N2-07","stem":"The resistivity of a material is related to resistance by R = ρL/A. Doubling the length of a wire will:","options":{"A":"Double the resistance","B":"Leave resistance unchanged","C":"Quarter the resistance","D":"Halve the resistance"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GPHYS-CIRCUITS","scaffold":"R = ρL/A, so resistance is proportional to length and inversely proportional to cross-sectional area. Doubling the length doubles R; doubling the diameter quadruples the area and so quarters R. Resistivity ρ is a property of the material, measured in Ω m, and varies with temperature — rising for metals and falling for semiconductors such as a thermistor.","reforge":{"stem":"The resistance of a thermistor as temperature rises typically:","options":{"A":"Increases sharply","B":"Stays constant","C":"Decreases","D":"Falls to zero"},"correct":"C"},"spec":"PHYS-2","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-phys-3.5"},{"id":"PHYS-N2-08","stem":"A potential divider with two equal resistors across a 12 V supply gives an output of:","options":{"A":"12 V","B":"6 V","C":"24 V","D":"3 V"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GPHYS-CIRCUITS","scaffold":"A potential divider splits the supply in the ratio of the resistances: Vout = Vin × R₂/(R₁ + R₂). With equal resistors the output is half the supply, 6 V. Replacing one resistor with an LDR or thermistor makes the output vary with light or temperature, which is how sensor circuits are built. Drawing current from the output alters the ratio, so the loading effect must be considered.","reforge":{"stem":"In a potential divider, replacing one fixed resistor with a light-dependent resistor makes the output vary with:","options":{"A":"Temperature","B":"Supply frequency","C":"Current direction","D":"Light intensity"},"correct":"D"},"spec":"PHYS-2","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-phys-3.5"},{"id":"PHYS-N2-09","stem":"The emf of a cell with internal resistance is given by ε = I(R + r). The term Ir represents:","options":{"A":"The terminal potential difference","B":"The energy stored in the cell","C":"The 'lost volts' inside the cell","D":"The power output"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GPHYS-CIRCUITS","scaffold":"Internal resistance causes energy dissipation inside the source, so terminal pd V = ε − Ir. Plotting V against I gives a straight line with intercept ε and gradient −r. As current increases the terminal pd falls, which is why a car's headlights dim when the starter motor draws a large current. Maximum power is delivered to the external circuit when R equals r.","reforge":{"stem":"A graph of terminal pd against current for a cell has gradient equal to:","options":{"A":"Minus the internal resistance","B":"The power output","C":"The external circuit resistance","D":"The emf"},"correct":"A"},"spec":"PHYS-2","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-phys-3.5"},{"id":"PHYS-N2-10","stem":"In a transverse wave, the oscillations are:","options":{"A":"Parallel to the direction in which energy travels","B":"In circles around the source","C":"Absent altogether","D":"Perpendicular to the direction of energy transfer"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GPHYS-WAVES","scaffold":"Transverse waves oscillate perpendicular to the direction of travel — light and all electromagnetic waves, water waves, and waves on a string. Longitudinal waves oscillate parallel to it, forming compressions and rarefactions, as in sound. Only transverse waves can be polarised, which is direct evidence that light is transverse and that sound is not.","reforge":{"stem":"Sound cannot be polarised because it is a:","options":{"A":"Transverse wave","B":"Longitudinal wave","C":"Standing wave","D":"Electromagnetic wave"},"correct":"B"},"spec":"PHYS-2","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-phys-3.6"},{"id":"PHYS-N2-11","stem":"A wave has frequency 50 Hz and wavelength 6.0 m. Its speed is:","options":{"A":"300 m s⁻¹","B":"8.3 m s⁻¹","C":"56 m s⁻¹","D":"0.12 m s⁻¹"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GPHYS-WAVES","scaffold":"The wave equation gives v = fλ = 50 × 6.0 = 300 m s⁻¹. Frequency is the number of complete oscillations per second in hertz, and is set by the source; wavelength is the distance between adjacent points in phase. When a wave passes into a new medium the frequency stays the same while the speed and wavelength change together, which is what causes refraction.","reforge":{"stem":"When a wave passes from one medium into another, the quantity that stays the same is its:","options":{"A":"Speed","B":"Wavelength","C":"Frequency","D":"Amplitude"},"correct":"C"},"spec":"PHYS-2","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-phys-3.3"},{"id":"PHYS-N2-12","stem":"Two waves are in antiphase. Their phase difference is:","options":{"A":"π/2 rad","B":"π rad","C":"0 rad","D":"2π rad"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GPHYS-WAVES","scaffold":"A phase difference of π radians, or 180°, means antiphase: a crest of one aligns with a trough of the other, giving destructive interference. A difference of 0 or 2π means in phase, giving constructive interference. Path difference relates to phase: a whole number of wavelengths gives constructive interference, an odd number of half wavelengths gives destructive.","reforge":{"stem":"Two coherent waves arrive with a path difference of exactly one wavelength. The interference is:","options":{"A":"Partially destructive","B":"Destructive","C":"Impossible to determine","D":"Constructive"},"correct":"D"},"spec":"PHYS-2","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-phys-3.3"},{"id":"PHYS-N2-13","stem":"For a stable interference pattern, the two sources must be:","options":{"A":"Sources of two different frequencies","B":"Very far apart","C":"Coherent, with constant phase","D":"Of unequal amplitude"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GPHYS-WAVES","scaffold":"Coherence means a constant phase relationship and therefore the same frequency, which is why laser light produces clear fringes while two separate lamps do not. Young's double slit experiment uses a single source split into two to guarantee coherence. Fringe spacing w = λD/s, so the pattern spreads with longer wavelength, greater screen distance, or narrower slit separation.","reforge":{"stem":"In Young's double slit experiment, increasing the slit separation makes the fringes:","options":{"A":"Closer together","B":"Disappear entirely","C":"Brighter but unchanged in spacing","D":"Wider apart"},"correct":"A"},"spec":"PHYS-2","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-phys-3.3"},{"id":"PHYS-N2-14","stem":"A stationary wave is formed when two waves of the same frequency travel:","options":{"A":"In the same direction","B":"At right angles","C":"In the same direction as each other","D":"In opposite directions"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GPHYS-WAVES","scaffold":"A stationary wave forms from two identical waves travelling in opposite directions, usually an incident wave and its reflection. Nodes are points of zero amplitude and antinodes points of maximum; the distance between adjacent nodes is half a wavelength. Unlike a progressive wave, no net energy is transferred along a stationary wave and all points between nodes oscillate in phase.","reforge":{"stem":"On a stationary wave, the distance between two adjacent nodes is:","options":{"A":"One wavelength","B":"Half a wavelength","C":"A quarter of a wavelength","D":"Two wavelengths"},"correct":"B"},"spec":"PHYS-2","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-phys-3.3"},{"id":"PHYS-N2-15","stem":"Total internal reflection can occur only when light travels:","options":{"A":"Dense to less dense, above the critical angle","B":"Through a vacuum","C":"Along the normal","D":"From a less dense medium into a more dense medium"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GPHYS-WAVES","scaffold":"Total internal reflection requires light to move from a higher to a lower refractive index medium, and the angle of incidence to exceed the critical angle, where sin C = n₂/n₁. Optical fibres exploit this with a core of higher index than the cladding, so light is confined. At exactly the critical angle the refracted ray travels along the boundary at 90°.","reforge":{"stem":"In an optical fibre, the cladding has a refractive index that is:","options":{"A":"Higher than the core","B":"Equal to the core","C":"Lower than the core","D":"Equal to air"},"correct":"C"},"spec":"PHYS-2","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-phys-3.5"},{"id":"PHYS-N2-16","stem":"Light passes from air into glass. Its speed:","options":{"A":"Stays the same","B":"Decreases","C":"Increases","D":"Falls to zero"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GPHYS-WAVES","scaffold":"Refractive index n = c ÷ v, so a medium with n greater than 1 slows light: glass with n = 1.5 gives v = 2.0 × 10⁸ m s⁻¹. Slowing at the boundary bends the ray towards the normal. Snell's law states n₁ sin θ₁ = n₂ sin θ₂. Frequency is unchanged, so the wavelength shortens in proportion to the speed.","reforge":{"stem":"Light enters glass of refractive index 1.5 from air. The ray bends:","options":{"A":"Away from the normal","B":"Along the boundary","C":"Straight back out","D":"Towards the normal"},"correct":"D"},"spec":"PHYS-2","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-phys-3.5"},{"id":"PHYS-N2-17","stem":"Diffraction of a wave through a gap is most pronounced when the gap width is:","options":{"A":"Much larger than the wavelength","B":"Exactly zero","C":"Comparable to the wavelength","D":"Unrelated to the wavelength"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GPHYS-WAVES","scaffold":"Diffraction is greatest when the gap is about the same size as the wavelength; a much wider gap gives little spreading. This is why sound, with wavelengths of around a metre, diffracts around doorways while light, at hundreds of nanometres, does not noticeably. A diffraction grating uses many slits to give sharp maxima at angles satisfying d sin θ = nλ.","reforge":{"stem":"For a diffraction grating, the positions of the maxima are given by:","options":{"A":"d sinθ=nλ","B":"n = c ÷ v","C":"n = c/v","D":"v = fλ"},"correct":"A"},"spec":"PHYS-2","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-phys-3.3"},{"id":"PHYS-N2-18","stem":"Polarisation of light provides evidence that light is:","options":{"A":"A longitudinal wave","B":"A stream of particles only","C":"Unable to travel in a vacuum","D":"A transverse wave"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GPHYS-WAVES","scaffold":"Only transverse waves can be polarised, because the oscillation has a direction perpendicular to travel that a filter can select. A polarising filter transmits one plane of oscillation; a second filter at 90° to the first blocks the light entirely. Applications include polarised sunglasses reducing glare from horizontal surfaces, and the use of polarising filters in photography and stress analysis.","reforge":{"stem":"Two polarising filters are placed with their axes at 90° to each other. The light transmitted is:","options":{"A":"Halved in intensity","B":"Reduced to zero","C":"Unchanged in intensity","D":"Doubled in intensity"},"correct":"B"},"spec":"PHYS-2","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-phys-3.3"},{"id":"PHYS-N2-19","stem":"The amplitude of a wave determines its:","options":{"A":"Energy, as amplitude squared","B":"Frequency of oscillation","C":"Wavelength","D":"Its speed through the given medium"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GPHYS-WAVES","scaffold":"Wave intensity is proportional to the square of the amplitude, so doubling the amplitude quadruples the energy carried. Amplitude is independent of frequency, wavelength and speed. For sound, greater amplitude means greater loudness; for light, greater brightness. Intensity also obeys an inverse square law with distance from a point source, since the energy spreads over a growing spherical area.","reforge":{"stem":"The intensity of radiation from a point source at distance r varies as:","options":{"A":"1 ÷ r, inversely with distance","B":"r","C":"1/r²","D":"r²"},"correct":"C"},"spec":"PHYS-2","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-phys-3.3"},{"id":"PHYS-N2-20","stem":"A simple pendulum undergoes simple harmonic motion. Its acceleration is:","options":{"A":"Constant in magnitude and direction","B":"Proportional to x, towards centre","C":"Constant in both magnitude and direction","D":"Zero at the extremes of motion"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GPHYS-OSCILLATIONS-THERMAL","scaffold":"Simple harmonic motion is defined by a = −ω²x: acceleration proportional to displacement and always directed towards equilibrium, hence the minus sign. Acceleration is therefore greatest at maximum displacement and zero at the centre, while velocity is greatest at the centre and zero at the extremes. The period of a simple pendulum is T = 2π√(l/g), independent of amplitude for small angles.","reforge":{"stem":"In simple harmonic motion, the velocity of the oscillator is greatest:","options":{"A":"At maximum displacement from centre","B":"Midway between them","C":"Constant throughout","D":"At the equilibrium position"},"correct":"D"},"spec":"PHYS-2","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-phys-3.6"},{"id":"PHYS-N2-21","stem":"The period of a mass–spring oscillator is given by T = 2π√(m/k). Increasing the mass will:","options":{"A":"Decrease the period","B":"Leave the period unchanged","C":"Increase the period","D":"Stop the oscillation"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GPHYS-OSCILLATIONS-THERMAL","scaffold":"T = 2π√(m/k), so period increases with the square root of mass and decreases with stiffer springs. Quadrupling the mass doubles the period. Note that amplitude does not appear, so for small oscillations the period is independent of how far the mass is displaced — this isochronous property is what made pendulums useful as clocks.","reforge":{"stem":"For a simple pendulum undergoing small oscillations, the period depends on:","options":{"A":"The length of the pendulum","B":"The amplitude of swing","C":"The material the bob is made from","D":"The mass of the bob"},"correct":"A"},"spec":"PHYS-2","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-phys-3.5"},{"id":"PHYS-N2-22","stem":"Resonance occurs when a system is driven at a frequency equal to its:","options":{"A":"Damping frequency","B":"Half the natural frequency","C":"Double the natural frequency","D":"Natural frequency"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GPHYS-OSCILLATIONS-THERMAL","scaffold":"At resonance the driving frequency matches the natural frequency, energy transfer to the system is maximum and amplitude peaks sharply. Damping reduces the peak amplitude and broadens the resonance curve, shifting the maximum slightly below the natural frequency. Engineers damp structures to avoid destructive resonance, while instruments and radio tuning circuits exploit it deliberately.","reforge":{"stem":"Increasing the damping of a resonating system will:","options":{"A":"Increase the peak amplitude","B":"Lower the peak, broaden it","C":"Raise the natural frequency","D":"Increase the peak amplitude sharply"},"correct":"B"},"spec":"PHYS-2","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-phys-3.5"},{"id":"PHYS-N2-23","stem":"The internal energy of a system is the sum of the:","options":{"A":"Kinetic and potential of particles","B":"Work done and heat supplied","C":"Work done on it plus heat supplied","D":"Chemical energies of its bonds"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GPHYS-OSCILLATIONS-THERMAL","scaffold":"Internal energy is the total randomly distributed kinetic and potential energy of the particles. Raising temperature increases the mean kinetic energy; changing state alters the potential energy while temperature stays constant, which is why a substance melting absorbs energy without getting hotter. Absolute temperature in kelvin is proportional to mean kinetic energy, so 0 K corresponds to minimum internal energy.","reforge":{"stem":"During melting, the temperature stays constant because the energy supplied changes the particles':","options":{"A":"Kinetic energy of the particles","B":"Mass","C":"Potential, breaking bonds","D":"Total number"},"correct":"C"},"spec":"PHYS-2","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-phys-3.6"},{"id":"PHYS-N2-24","stem":"The specific heat capacity of a substance is the energy needed to raise the temperature of:","options":{"A":"1 mole by 1 K","B":"1 kg by 1 K","C":"Any mass by 1 K","D":"1 kg through a change of state"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GPHYS-OSCILLATIONS-THERMAL","scaffold":"Specific heat capacity c is energy per kilogram per kelvin, used in Q = mcΔT. Specific latent heat is the energy per kilogram to change state at constant temperature, used in Q = mL, with separate values for fusion and vaporisation. A common error is applying mcΔT during a change of state, when the temperature is not changing at all and mL applies instead.","reforge":{"stem":"To calculate the energy needed to boil water already at 100 °C, the correct equation is:","options":{"A":"Q = mcΔT","B":"Q = mgh","C":"Q = ½mv²","D":"Q = mL"},"correct":"D"},"spec":"PHYS-2","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-phys-3.6"},{"id":"PHYS-N2-25","stem":"For an ideal gas at constant temperature, pressure and volume are related by:","options":{"A":"p ∝ V","B":"p ∝ V²","C":"p ∝ 1/V","D":"p is independent of V"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GPHYS-OSCILLATIONS-THERMAL","scaffold":"Boyle's law states pV = constant at fixed temperature, so pressure is inversely proportional to volume. Charles's law gives V ∝ T at constant pressure, and the pressure law p ∝ T at constant volume, both with T in kelvin. Combining them gives the ideal gas equation pV = nRT. Converting Celsius to kelvin by adding 273 is essential; using Celsius directly produces nonsense.","reforge":{"stem":"In the ideal gas equation pV = nRT, the temperature must be expressed in:","options":{"A":"Kelvin","B":"Degrees Fahrenheit","C":"Any consistent unit","D":"Degrees Celsius"},"correct":"A"},"spec":"PHYS-2","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-phys-3.6"},{"id":"PHYS-N2-26","stem":"A magnetic field exerts a force on a current-carrying wire. The force is greatest when the wire is:","options":{"A":"Aligned parallel to the field","B":"At 45° to the field","C":"Carrying no current","D":"Perpendicular to the field"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GPHYS-FIELDS-ELECTROMAGNETISM","scaffold":"F = BIL sin θ, so the force is maximum when the wire is perpendicular to the field (θ = 90°) and zero when parallel. Fleming's left-hand rule gives the direction: first finger field, second finger current, thumb motion. Magnetic flux density B is measured in tesla, where one tesla produces one newton per amp per metre on a perpendicular conductor.","reforge":{"stem":"A wire carries 3.0 A perpendicular to a field of 0.20 T over a length of 0.50 m. The force is:","options":{"A":"3.7 N","B":"0.30 N","C":"0.033 N","D":"1.2 N"},"correct":"B"},"spec":"PHYS-2","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-phys-3.7"},{"id":"PHYS-N2-27","stem":"Faraday's law states that the induced emf is proportional to the rate of change of:","options":{"A":"Magnetic flux linkage","B":"Charge","C":"Current flowing in the coil","D":"Current"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GPHYS-FIELDS-ELECTROMAGNETISM","scaffold":"Faraday's law gives ε = −N dΦ/dt, so a faster change of flux, or more turns, induces a larger emf. Lenz's law supplies the minus sign: the induced current opposes the change producing it, which is a statement of energy conservation. Flux Φ = BA cos θ, and flux linkage is NΦ. A stationary magnet in a coil induces nothing, since the flux is not changing.","reforge":{"stem":"The minus sign in Faraday's law expresses Lenz's law, which follows from conservation of:","options":{"A":"Charge","B":"Momentum","C":"Energy","D":"Magnetic flux"},"correct":"C"},"spec":"PHYS-2","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-phys-3.5"},{"id":"PHYS-N2-28","stem":"A transformer works on alternating current but not direct current because it requires:","options":{"A":"A high resistance in the primary","B":"A continuously changing magnetic flux","C":"A very high frequency supply","D":"A very high frequency alternating supply"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GPHYS-FIELDS-ELECTROMAGNETISM","scaffold":"A transformer relies on a changing flux in the core inducing an emf in the secondary, so a steady direct current produces nothing after the initial switch-on. The turns ratio gives Vs/Vp = Ns/Np. For an ideal transformer power is conserved, so stepping voltage up steps current down. Real losses come from resistance in the windings, eddy currents, and hysteresis in the core.","reforge":{"stem":"A transformer has 100 primary turns and 400 secondary turns with 12 V input. The output voltage is:","options":{"A":"3 V","B":"12 V","C":"400 V","D":"48 V"},"correct":"D"},"spec":"PHYS-2","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-phys-3.5"},{"id":"PHYS-N2-29","stem":"Eddy currents in a transformer core are reduced by:","options":{"A":"Using a solid iron core","B":"Increasing the supply voltage","C":"Laminating the core","D":"Removing the core entirely"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GPHYS-FIELDS-ELECTROMAGNETISM","scaffold":"Laminating the core with insulated layers breaks the paths available to eddy currents, cutting the heat they generate. Other transformer losses: resistive heating in the windings, reduced by thicker low-resistance wire; and hysteresis loss, reduced by using a soft magnetic material that magnetises and demagnetises easily. Removing the core would drastically reduce flux linkage and so the induced emf.","reforge":{"stem":"Resistive heating in transformer windings is reduced by using wire that is:","options":{"A":"Thicker, lower resistance","B":"Made from a poorer conductor","C":"Wound in fewer turns","D":"Thinner and longer"},"correct":"A"},"spec":"PHYS-2","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-phys-3.5"},{"id":"PHYS-N2-30","stem":"The root mean square value of an alternating voltage is related to its peak value by:","options":{"A":"Vrms = Vpeak","B":"Vrms = Vpeak × √2","C":"Vrms = Vpeak ÷ 2","D":"Vrms = Vpeak ÷ √2"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GPHYS-FIELDS-ELECTROMAGNETISM","scaffold":"For a sinusoidal supply, Vrms = Vpeak ÷ √2, and likewise for current. The rms value is the direct current equivalent that would deliver the same average power, which is why mains electricity quoted as 230 V is an rms value with a peak of about 325 V. Average power is VrmsIrms, half the peak power.","reforge":{"stem":"UK mains is quoted as 230 V. Its peak voltage is approximately:","options":{"A":"163 V","B":"325 V","C":"230 V","D":"460 V"},"correct":"B"},"spec":"PHYS-2","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-phys-3.5"},{"id":"PHYS-N2-31","stem":"A charged particle moves parallel to a magnetic field. The magnetic force on it is:","options":{"A":"Zero","B":"Equal to BQv","C":"Directed along the field","D":"Maximum"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GPHYS-FIELDS-ELECTROMAGNETISM","scaffold":"F = BQv sin θ, so a particle moving parallel to the field (θ = 0) feels no force, while one moving perpendicular feels the maximum BQv. That perpendicular force is always at right angles to the velocity, so it does no work and the speed is unchanged — the particle follows a circular path of radius r = mv/BQ. This is the basis of the cyclotron and the mass spectrometer.","reforge":{"stem":"A charged particle enters a magnetic field perpendicular to it. Its subsequent path is:","options":{"A":"A straight line","B":"A parabola","C":"Circular","D":"A spiral inwards"},"correct":"C"},"spec":"PHYS-2","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-phys-3.7"},{"id":"PHYS-N2-32","stem":"Electric field strength at a point is best described as the:","options":{"A":"Force per unit mass","B":"Force per unit charge","C":"Energy per unit charge","D":"Charge per unit area"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GPHYS-FIELDS-ELECTROMAGNETISM","scaffold":"Electric field strength E = F/Q in N C⁻¹ or V m⁻¹. Around a point charge E = kQ/r², an inverse square law like gravitation, but electric forces can attract or repel while gravity only attracts. Between parallel plates the field is uniform, with E = V/d. Field lines run from positive to negative and are perpendicular to the conductor surface.","reforge":{"stem":"Two parallel plates 0.02 m apart have a pd of 200 V. The field strength between them is:","options":{"A":"4 V m⁻¹","B":"0.0001 V m⁻¹","C":"200 V m⁻¹","D":"10,000 V m⁻¹"},"correct":"D"},"spec":"PHYS-2","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-phys-3.7"},{"id":"PHYS-N2-33","stem":"A capacitor stores energy in the form of:","options":{"A":"A magnetic field around its plates","B":"Chemical energy","C":"An electric field between plates","D":"Kinetic energy of electrons"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GPHYS-FIELDS-ELECTROMAGNETISM","scaffold":"Capacitance C = Q/V in farads, and the energy stored is W = ½QV = ½CV². The half arises because the pd rises as charge accumulates, so the average pd during charging is half the final value. On a graph of charge against pd the area beneath gives the energy. During discharge through a resistor the charge decays exponentially with time constant τ = RC.","reforge":{"stem":"A capacitor discharges through a resistor. The time constant of the circuit is:","options":{"A":"RC","B":"C/R","C":"1/RC","D":"R/C"},"correct":"A"},"spec":"PHYS-2","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-phys-3.5"},{"id":"PHYS-N2-34","stem":"After one time constant, the charge remaining on a discharging capacitor falls to about:","options":{"A":"50% of the initial value","B":"63% of the initial value","C":"Zero","D":"37% of the initial value"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GPHYS-FIELDS-ELECTROMAGNETISM","scaffold":"Exponential decay follows Q = Q₀e^(−t/RC). After one time constant t = RC, so Q = Q₀e⁻¹ ≈ 0.37Q₀ — about 37% remains, meaning 63% has been lost. The same fraction applies to current and pd. Because the decay is exponential the charge never reaches exactly zero, and the half-life of the decay is 0.69RC.","reforge":{"stem":"Doubling the resistance in a capacitor discharge circuit makes the discharge:","options":{"A":"Twice as fast","B":"Twice as slow","C":"Unchanged in rate","D":"Instantaneous"},"correct":"B"},"spec":"PHYS-2","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-phys-3.5"},{"id":"PHYS-N2-35","stem":"A step-up transformer increases voltage. The current in the secondary coil is therefore:","options":{"A":"Decreased, since power is conserved","B":"Reduced to zero","C":"Unchanged","D":"Increased in exactly the same ratio"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GPHYS-FIELDS-ELECTROMAGNETISM","scaffold":"For an ideal transformer, input power equals output power: VpIp = VsIs. Stepping the voltage up by a factor therefore steps the current down by the same factor. This is what makes long-distance transmission efficient, since power lost in cables is I²R and reducing the current has a squared effect. Transformers cannot increase power — only trade voltage against current.","reforge":{"stem":"An ideal transformer steps 240 V up to 960 V. If the primary current is 8.0 A, the secondary current is:","options":{"A":"32 A","B":"8.0 A","C":"2.0 A","D":"0.5 A"},"correct":"C"},"spec":"PHYS-2","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-phys-3.5"},{"id":"PHYS-N2-36","stem":"In a circuit, a component with a very high resistance placed in parallel with another will:","options":{"A":"Carry most of the current","B":"Carry very little current","C":"Carry almost all of the current","D":"Stop all current flowing"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GPHYS-CIRCUITS","scaffold":"In parallel each branch has the same pd, so by I = V/R the branch with higher resistance carries the smaller current — current takes the path of least resistance in that proportional sense. Adding any parallel branch lowers the total resistance, since it adds another route for charge. Voltmeters are designed with very high resistance so they draw negligible current from the circuit they measure.","reforge":{"stem":"An ideal voltmeter has very high resistance so that it:","options":{"A":"Reduces the supply voltage","B":"Increases the total circuit current","C":"Protects the battery","D":"Draws negligible current"},"correct":"D"},"spec":"PHYS-2","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-phys-3.5"},{"id":"PHYS-N2-37","stem":"An ammeter should be connected in a circuit:","options":{"A":"In parallel with the component","B":"Across the battery only","C":"In series with the component","D":"In place of the battery"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GPHYS-CIRCUITS","scaffold":"An ammeter measures current, so it must be in series to carry the same current as the component, and it is designed with very low resistance to avoid altering the circuit. A voltmeter measures pd across a component and so goes in parallel, with very high resistance. Connecting an ammeter in parallel across a supply creates a near short circuit and can damage it.","reforge":{"stem":"An ideal ammeter has very low resistance so that it:","options":{"A":"Does not reduce circuit current","B":"Protects the circuit against short circuits","C":"Can be used as a voltmeter","D":"Increases the current measured"},"correct":"A"},"spec":"PHYS-2","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-phys-3.5"},{"id":"PHYS-N2-38","stem":"The number of electrons passing a point per second is doubled. The current:","options":{"A":"Halves","B":"Stays the same","C":"Quadruples","D":"Doubles"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GPHYS-CIRCUITS","scaffold":"Current is the rate of flow of charge, so doubling the number of charge carriers passing per second doubles the current. In terms of drift velocity, I = nAvq, where n is the number density of carriers, A the cross-sectional area, v the drift velocity and q the charge on each. Drift velocity is surprisingly slow, typically under a millimetre per second, although the electric field is established almost instantly.","reforge":{"stem":"In the equation I = nAvq, the symbol v represents the:","options":{"A":"Volume of the conductor","B":"Drift velocity of the carriers","C":"Voltage across the wire","D":"Speed of the electric field itself"},"correct":"B"},"spec":"PHYS-2","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-phys-3.5"},{"id":"PHYS-N2-39","stem":"Sound travels faster in solids than in gases mainly because in solids the particles are:","options":{"A":"More closely spaced and strongly bonded","B":"Electrically charged rather than neutral","C":"Moving more quickly","D":"More massive"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GPHYS-WAVES","scaffold":"Sound is a longitudinal mechanical wave transmitted by particle interaction, so closer spacing and stronger bonds pass the disturbance on faster: roughly 340 m s⁻¹ in air, 1500 m s⁻¹ in water and 5000 m s⁻¹ in steel. Sound cannot travel through a vacuum at all, since there are no particles — unlike electromagnetic waves, which need no medium.","reforge":{"stem":"Sound cannot travel through a vacuum because it requires:","options":{"A":"A magnetic field","B":"A high temperature","C":"A material medium","D":"An electric charge"},"correct":"C"},"spec":"PHYS-2","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-phys-3.2"},{"id":"PHYS-N2-40","stem":"A wave's frequency is 250 Hz. Its period is:","options":{"A":"250 s","B":"0.004 s","C":"4.0 s","D":"0.25 seconds"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GPHYS-WAVES","scaffold":"Period T = 1/f = 1/250 = 0.004 s, or 4 ms. Period is the time for one complete oscillation, frequency the number per second, so they are reciprocals. Getting them the wrong way round produces answers out by a large factor, so a quick sanity check helps: a high frequency must correspond to a very short period.","reforge":{"stem":"A wave has a period of 0.02 s. Its frequency is:","options":{"A":"0.02 Hz","B":"20 Hz","C":"2 Hz","D":"50 Hz"},"correct":"D"},"spec":"PHYS-2","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-phys-3.3"},{"id":"PHYS-N2-41","stem":"Two identical resistors in parallel are connected to a supply. Compared with one resistor alone, the total current drawn is:","options":{"A":"Halved","B":"Unchanged","C":"Doubled","D":"Quartered"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GPHYS-CIRCUITS","scaffold":"Adding a parallel branch halves the total resistance, and since the supply pd is unchanged, I = V/R means the total current doubles. Each branch still carries the same current it would have alone. This is why adding appliances to a household ring main increases total current draw and why circuits are fused — too many parallel loads can exceed the safe current.","reforge":{"stem":"Adding more components in parallel to a supply causes the total resistance to:","options":{"A":"Decrease","B":"Become infinite","C":"Stay the same","D":"Increase"},"correct":"A"},"spec":"PHYS-2","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-phys-3.5"},{"id":"PHYS-N2-42","stem":"The damping of an oscillating system that returns to equilibrium in the shortest time without overshooting is described as:","options":{"A":"Light damping of the system","B":"Heavy damping","C":"No damping","D":"Critical damping"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GPHYS-OSCILLATIONS-THERMAL","scaffold":"Light damping gives gradually decaying oscillations; critical damping returns the system to equilibrium in the shortest time with no oscillation; heavy or over-damping returns it slowly without oscillating. Critical damping is what car suspension and analogue meter needles aim for. Damping always removes energy from the system, reducing amplitude while leaving the natural frequency almost unchanged.","reforge":{"stem":"Car suspension systems are designed to be close to critically damped so that the car:","options":{"A":"Oscillates for a long time after every bump","B":"Returns to level quickly without bouncing","C":"Never moves at all","D":"Resonates with the road surface"},"correct":"B"},"spec":"PHYS-2","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-phys-3.5"},{"id":"PHYS-N2-43","stem":"A gas is compressed rapidly with no heat exchange. Its internal energy:","options":{"A":"Increases: work is done on it","B":"Falls to zero","C":"Stays constant during the compression","D":"Decreases"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GPHYS-OSCILLATIONS-THERMAL","scaffold":"The first law of thermodynamics states ΔU = Q + W: the change in internal energy equals heat supplied plus work done on the system. With no heat transfer, compressing the gas does work on it, so internal energy and therefore temperature rise — which is why a bicycle pump warms up. Expansion by the gas does work on the surroundings and cools it.","reforge":{"stem":"A gas expands and does work on its surroundings with no heat supplied. Its temperature:","options":{"A":"Rises","B":"Stays constant","C":"Falls","D":"Doubles"},"correct":"C"},"spec":"PHYS-2","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-phys-3.6"},{"id":"PHYS-N2-44","stem":"Two coherent sources produce a path difference of half a wavelength at a point. The result there is:","options":{"A":"A maximum of intensity","B":"A minimum of intensity","C":"No wave at all arriving","D":"A change of frequency"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GPHYS-WAVES","scaffold":"A path difference of an odd number of half wavelengths puts the waves in antiphase, so they interfere destructively and the intensity is a minimum — zero if the amplitudes are equal. A whole number of wavelengths gives constructive interference and a maximum. Energy is not destroyed at a minimum; it is redistributed to the maxima elsewhere in the pattern.","reforge":{"stem":"At a point of destructive interference, the energy that would have arrived is:","options":{"A":"Destroyed","B":"Converted to heat","C":"Reflected back to the source","D":"Redistributed to the maxima"},"correct":"D"},"spec":"PHYS-2","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-phys-3.3"},{"id":"PHYS-N2-45","stem":"Increasing the temperature of a fixed mass of gas at constant volume causes the pressure to:","options":{"A":"Fall, since the particles spread further out","B":"Stay the same","C":"Rise: collisions more frequent","D":"Fall to zero at 100 °C"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GPHYS-OSCILLATIONS-THERMAL","scaffold":"Kinetic theory explains pressure as the result of particle collisions with the container walls. Raising temperature increases mean particle speed, so collisions are both more frequent and more forceful, raising pressure — the pressure law, p ∝ T in kelvin. Extrapolating a pressure–temperature graph back to zero pressure gives absolute zero, −273 °C, which is how that value is determined experimentally.","reforge":{"stem":"Extrapolating a graph of gas pressure against Celsius temperature to zero pressure gives:","options":{"A":"−273 °C","B":"100 degrees","C":"273 °C","D":"0 °C"},"correct":"A"},"spec":"PHYS-2","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-phys-3.5"}]},"PHYS-3":{"label":"Electricity, Waves & Quantum","color":"#1e3a8a","questions":[{"id":"EWQ-01","stem":"A student claims that current is 'used up' as it flows around a series circuit, so less current returns to the battery than leaves it. What is wrong?","options":{"A":"Charge is conserved, so current is the same throughout","B":"The student is correct — current does decrease around a series circuit.","C":"Current increases around a series circuit because the battery replenishes it.","D":"Current is used up, but only in resistors — not in wires."},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GPHYS-CIRCUITS","scaffold":"Kirchhoff's First Law: the sum of currents entering a junction equals the sum leaving (conservation of charge). In a series circuit there are no junctions — current is the same everywhere. Confusion arises because voltage drops across components: V = IR means each resistor 'uses' voltage, not current. Students confuse the dimming of bulbs in series (because voltage is shared) with current being consumed.","reforge":{"stem":"Two identical bulbs are connected in series with a battery. Bulb A is closer to the positive terminal. Which burns brighter?","options":{"A":"Parallel circuits always have lower resistance than series circuits regardless of component values.","B":"Bulb B — current builds up as it flows around.","C":"They are equally bright — the same current flows through both in a series circuit.","D":"Neither — bulbs in series always go out."},"correct":"C"},"spec":"PHYS-3","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-phys-3.5"},{"id":"EWQ-02","stem":"The resistance of a thermistor decreases as temperature increases. In a potential divider circuit with a fixed resistor, how does the output voltage across the thermistor change as it warms up?","options":{"A":"Output voltage increases — lower resistance means more current, so more voltage.","B":"Output voltage decreases","C":"Output voltage stays constant — the supply voltage is fixed.","D":"Output voltage increases only if the thermistor is above 25°C."},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GPHYS-CIRCUITS","scaffold":"Potential divider: V_out = V_supply × R_thermistor/(R_fixed + R_thermistor). As temperature rises, R_thermistor falls → numerator falls → V_out falls. This is the basis of temperature sensors. Exam trap: students think lower resistance → more current → more voltage. True that current increases (I = V/R_total), but V_out = IR_thermistor — the larger I is multiplied by a much smaller R, giving a smaller V_out overall.","reforge":{"stem":"In the same potential divider, the output is taken across the FIXED resistor instead. What happens to this output as the thermistor warms up?","options":{"A":"Output decreases — fixed resistor has lower share.","B":"Output stays the same — it is a fixed resistor.","C":"Output decreases because total resistance falls.","D":"Output increases as the fixed resistor takes more"},"correct":"D"},"spec":"PHYS-3","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-phys-3.5"},{"id":"EWQ-03","stem":"A wave has frequency 500 Hz and wavelength 0.68 m. What is its wave speed?","options":{"A":"735 m/s","B":"0.00136 m/s","C":"340 m/s","D":"500.68 m/s"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GPHYS-WAVES","scaffold":"Wave equation: v = fλ = 500 × 0.68 = 340 m/s. This is the speed of sound in air at room temperature — a useful sense-check. Common errors: (1) dividing instead of multiplying (f/λ gives 735 — wrong); (2) adding f and λ; (3) not recognising the answer as physically plausible. Always check units and order of magnitude: sound ~340 m/s, light ~3×10⁸ m/s.","reforge":{"stem":"A radio wave travels at 3×10⁸ m/s and has a frequency of 100 MHz (10⁸ Hz). What is its wavelength?","options":{"A":"3 m","B":"3×10¹⁶ m","C":"0.33 m","D":"3×10⁻⁸ m"},"correct":"A"},"spec":"PHYS-3","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-phys-3.3"},{"id":"EWQ-04","stem":"Young's double-slit experiment produces an interference pattern of bright and dark fringes. What does this pattern demonstrate?","options":{"A":"That light travels in straight lines.","B":"That light is a stream of particles that pass through both slits simultaneously.","C":"That light can only travel through very narrow gaps.","D":"The wave nature of light, shown by interference"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GPHYS-WAVES","scaffold":"Young's double-slit (1801): light from two coherent sources interferes. Bright fringe: waves arrive in phase (path difference = whole number of wavelengths). Dark fringe: waves arrive in antiphase (path difference = half-integer wavelengths). The existence of dark fringes (where light + light = no light) can only be explained by wave superposition — a particle model cannot produce this. This was key evidence for the wave theory of light before quantum mechanics complicated matters.","reforge":{"stem":"The fringe spacing in Young's experiment is w = λD/d. A student increases the slit separation d while keeping all else constant. What happens to the fringe spacing?","options":{"A":"Fringe spacing increases — wider slits diffract more.","B":"Fringe spacing decreases — w = λD/d, so increasing d reduces w. The fringes get closer together.","C":"Removing every value that differs from the rest removes uncertainty under standard test conditions","D":"Fringe spacing increases because more light gets through."},"correct":"B"},"spec":"PHYS-3","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-phys-3.3"},{"id":"EWQ-05","stem":"The photoelectric effect could not be explained by the wave model of light. Which observation specifically disproved the wave model?","options":{"A":"Below a threshold frequency no electrons are ejected","B":"Light ejects electrons more quickly from metals than from non-metals.","C":"Electrons are only emitted when light is focused with a lens.","D":"The photoelectric effect only occurs with visible light, not with radio waves."},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GPHYS-QUANTUM-NUCLEAR","scaffold":"Wave model prediction: energy delivered continuously — given enough time or intensity, any frequency should eject electrons, and higher intensity should increase electron energy. Observations that contradict this: (1) threshold frequency exists — below it, no electrons however intense the light; (2) above threshold, electron energy depends on frequency, not intensity; (3) emission is instantaneous even at low intensity. Einstein's photon model explains all three: photon energy E = hf; if hf < work function φ, no emission.","reforge":{"stem":"Why does increasing light intensity above the threshold frequency increase the number of photoelectrons but not their maximum kinetic energy?","options":{"A":"Intensity determines how fast electrons move through the metal.","B":"Above the threshold, the work function increases with intensity, when in fact the work function is a fixed property of the metal that never changes with the intensity of the incoming light, only with which metal is used.","C":"Higher intensity means more photons per second, each with the same energy (E = hf). More photons eject more electrons, but each photon still transfers the same energy — so KE_max = hf − φ is unchanged.","D":"Intensity and frequency both affect electron energy equally."},"correct":"C"},"spec":"PHYS-3","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-phys-3.12"},{"id":"EWQ-06","stem":"A student correctly calculates that total resistance of two resistors in parallel is LESS than either individual resistor. Why is this?","options":{"A":"Parallel connection reduces the voltage across each resistor.","B":"A parallel path adds another route for current","C":"Parallel circuits always have lower resistance than series circuits regardless of component values.","D":"Resistors in parallel cancel each other's resistance."},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GPHYS-CIRCUITS","scaffold":"Parallel resistors: 1/R_total = 1/R₁ + 1/R₂. Each parallel branch carries its own current. Total current = sum of branch currents. Since V is the same across both, and I_total = I₁ + I₂ > either branch current, R_total = V/I_total < V/I₁ or V/I₂. Intuition: adding a parallel path is like adding lanes to a motorway — more flow for the same 'pressure'. Always less than smallest individual resistor.","reforge":{"stem":"Three identical resistors each of 12 Ω are connected in parallel. What is the total resistance?","options":{"A":"36 Ω — the three resistances added directly.","B":"Output voltage increases — lower resistance means more current, so more voltage.","C":"12 Ω — the value of one resistor unchanged.","D":"4 Ω — for parallel resistors: 1/R = 1/12 + 1/12 + 1/12 = 3/12, giving R = 4 Ω."},"correct":"D"},"spec":"PHYS-3","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-phys-3.5"},{"id":"EWQ-07","stem":"Stationary (standing) waves form when two waves of equal frequency and amplitude travel in opposite directions. Which statement correctly describes a node?","options":{"A":"A node is a point of maximum displacement where the two waves always constructively interfere.","B":"A node only forms at the ends of a string.","C":"A node is a point of zero displacement","D":"Nodes and antinodes alternate with the same separation as the wavelength."},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GPHYS-WAVES","scaffold":"Stationary wave: superposition of two equal waves travelling in opposite directions. Nodes: fixed points of zero displacement — destructive interference always (path difference = (n+½)λ for the two sources). Antinodes: points of maximum displacement — constructive interference always. Node-to-node distance = λ/2 (not λ). Exam trap: (1) stating nodes are at maximum displacement; (2) stating node-to-node distance = λ. Evidence for stationary waves: nodes are truly stationary, unlike in a progressive wave where all points oscillate.","reforge":{"stem":"A guitar string of length 0.65 m vibrates in its fundamental mode (one antinode in the middle). What is the wavelength of the stationary wave?","options":{"A":"1.3 m — the wavelength is twice the string length for the fundamental mode.","B":"Particles remain completely stationary in all wave types — only energy moves.","C":"0.65 m — wavelength equals string length.","D":"1.95 m — three half-wavelengths fit."},"correct":"A"},"spec":"PHYS-3","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-phys-3.3"},{"id":"EWQ-08","stem":"An electron is accelerated through a potential difference of 1000 V. Using de Broglie's equation λ = h/mv, which statement about its wavelength is correct?","options":{"A":"The electron has no wavelength — only photons have wavelengths.","B":"The electron's wavelength is around 550 nm — the same as visible light.","C":"The wavelength increases as the electron is accelerated to higher speeds.","D":"The electron wavelength is far shorter than light"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GPHYS-QUANTUM-NUCLEAR","scaffold":"de Broglie: λ = h/p = h/mv. For an electron accelerated through V: KE = eV = ½mv² → v = √(2eV/m). With V=1000V: v ≈ 1.9×10⁷ m/s, λ ≈ 3.9×10⁻¹¹ m ≈ 0.04 nm. Visible light: 400–700 nm. The electron wavelength is ~10,000× shorter → can resolve atomic-scale features. Higher accelerating voltage → higher KE → higher momentum → shorter wavelength (λ = h/p, p larger). Used in electron microscopes and supports wave-particle duality.","reforge":{"stem":"Why does increasing the accelerating voltage of an electron microscope improve its resolution?","options":{"A":"Higher voltage produces brighter electrons that illuminate samples better.","B":"Higher voltage increases electron energy and momentum — since λ = h/p, greater momentum means shorter de Broglie wavelength, and shorter wavelength allows resolution of smaller features (resolution limit ≈ wavelength).","C":"Higher voltage increases the number of electrons, improving image brightness. The physical explanation must use the stated quantities, units, forces, energy transfers and assumptions; the option confuses a definition with its application to the specific example.","D":"Resolution depends on the detector, not the electron wavelength."},"correct":"B"},"spec":"PHYS-3","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-phys-3.12"},{"id":"PHYS-E3-01","spec":"PHYS-3","stem":"The standard model describes:","options":{"A":"fundamental particles and their interactions","B":"only planetary motion","C":"The time for every nucleus in the sample to decay under standard conditions","D":"the design of telescopes"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GPHYS-QUANTUM-NUCLEAR","scaffold":"It classifies matter particles and force-carrying particles, excluding gravity in its basic form.","reforge":{"stem":"Which theory classifies quarks, leptons and the force-carrying bosons and describes how they interact?","options":{"A":"The standard model of particle physics","B":"A theory describing only the orbits of planets","C":"A theory describing every chemical reaction rate","D":"An engineering theory for designing telescopes"},"correct":"A"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-phys-3.2"},{"id":"PHYS-E3-02","spec":"PHYS-3","stem":"In the photoelectric effect, increasing light frequency above threshold increases:","options":{"A":"Electrons are only emitted when light is focused with a lens.","B":"the maximum kinetic energy of emitted electrons","C":"the electron charge","D":"the wavelength of the light"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GPHYS-QUANTUM-NUCLEAR","scaffold":"Photon energy hf increases with frequency; intensity mainly affects emission rate.","reforge":{"stem":"Two identical metal surfaces are hit by light above the threshold frequency, one at a higher frequency than the other. What differs between the emitted electrons?","options":{"A":"The higher-frequency light changes the metal's work function","B":"The higher-frequency light produces electrons with greater maximum kinetic energy","C":"The higher-frequency light changes the electron's charge","D":"The higher-frequency light increases the wavelength of the incident beam under standard test conditions"},"correct":"B"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-phys-3.12"},{"id":"PHYS-E3-03","spec":"PHYS-3","stem":"A diffraction grating produces principal maxima when:","options":{"A":"Transverse waves travel through space but cannot transfer energy to matter.","B":"the slits are closed","C":"path difference is an integer multiple of wavelength","D":"the frequency is zero"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GPHYS-WAVES","scaffold":"Constructive interference occurs for nλ = d sin θ.","reforge":{"stem":"At which angles does a diffraction grating produce bright fringes?","options":{"A":"Where the path difference is always exactly half a wavelength under standard test conditions","B":"Where every slit in the grating is blocked","C":"Where the path difference between adjacent slits equals a whole number of wavelengths","D":"Where the light's frequency falls to zero"},"correct":"C"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-phys-3.3"},{"id":"PHYS-E3-04","spec":"PHYS-3","stem":"An astronomical parallax angle can be used to determine:","options":{"A":"the star's chemical formula only","B":"the age of the universe directly","C":"the speed of light","D":"distance to a nearby star"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GPHYS-ASTRONOMY","scaffold":"Small apparent shifts against distant background objects give stellar distance.","reforge":{"stem":"Astronomers measure how much a nearby star appears to shift against distant background stars over six months. What can this shift be used to calculate?","options":{"A":"The distance to that nearby star","B":"The star's exact chemical composition","C":"The age of the entire universe","D":"The speed of light in a vacuum"},"correct":"A"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-phys-3.9"},{"id":"PHYS-E3-05","spec":"PHYS-3","stem":"The luminosity of a star is its:","options":{"A":"total power output","B":"apparent angular size","C":"surface temperature only","D":"distance from Earth"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GPHYS-ASTRONOMY","scaffold":"Luminosity is intrinsic power, while apparent brightness also depends on distance.","reforge":{"stem":"Which property of a star describes the total amount of energy it radiates every second?","options":{"A":"Its apparent angular size as seen from Earth under standard test conditions","B":"Its luminosity, the star's total power output","C":"Its surface temperature alone","D":"Its distance from Earth"},"correct":"B"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-phys-3.9"},{"id":"PHYS-E3-06","spec":"PHYS-3","stem":"A practical conclusion is more reliable when:","options":{"A":"Rounding the final answer to fewer significant figures under standard test conditions","B":"repeated measurements are consistent and uncertainties are considered","C":"units are omitted","D":"variables are changed together"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GPHYS-MEASUREMENT","scaffold":"Reliability improves through repeats, control of variables and evaluation of uncertainty.","reforge":{"stem":"An experiment is repeated several times, giving closely grouped results, and the uncertainty in each reading is stated. What does this support?","options":{"A":"A conclusion based on a single anomalous result","B":"A conclusion drawn without recording any units","C":"A more reliable conclusion, since precision and uncertainty were considered","D":"A conclusion where variables were changed together under standard test conditions"},"correct":"C"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-phys-3.1"},{"id":"PHYS-E3-07","spec":"PHYS-3","stem":"In a nuclear fission reaction, a heavy nucleus:","options":{"A":"combines with an electron only","B":"The photoelectric effect only occurs with visible light, not with radio waves.","C":"splits into smaller nuclei and releases energy","D":"becomes a chemical compound"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GPHYS-QUANTUM-NUCLEAR","scaffold":"Fission can release neutrons and energy, enabling a chain reaction.","reforge":{"stem":"A uranium nucleus absorbs a neutron and then breaks apart into two lighter nuclei, releasing energy. What process is this?","options":{"A":"Nuclear fission, a heavy nucleus splitting and releasing energy","B":"A reaction where the nucleus only combines with an electron under standard test conditions","C":"The nucleus emitting visible light without changing","D":"The nucleus becoming a chemical compound"},"correct":"A"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-phys-3.8"},{"id":"PHYS-E3-08","spec":"PHYS-3","stem":"The threshold frequency in the photoelectric effect is determined by:","options":{"A":"the brightness of the source only","B":"the distance from the lamp","C":"the electron's rest mass only","D":"the work function of the metal"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GPHYS-QUANTUM-NUCLEAR","scaffold":"Below threshold, individual photons do not have enough energy to release electrons.","reforge":{"stem":"Why does a metal's threshold frequency for the photoelectric effect vary between different metals?","options":{"A":"Because it depends only on how bright the light source is","B":"Because it depends on the metal's work function","C":"Because it depends only on the distance from the light source","D":"Because it depends only on the electron's rest mass"},"correct":"B"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-phys-3.12"},{"id":"PHYS-E3B-01","spec":"PHYS-3","stem":"The de Broglie wavelength of a particle decreases when its momentum:","options":{"A":"increases","B":"decreases to zero","C":"becomes negative only","D":"is replaced by its charge"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GPHYS-QUANTUM-NUCLEAR","scaffold":"λ = h/p, so greater momentum gives a shorter wavelength.","reforge":{"stem":"According to the de Broglie relation, what happens to a particle's wavelength as its momentum gets larger?","options":{"A":"The wavelength becomes zero at every momentum","B":"The wavelength becomes negative","C":"The wavelength gets smaller, since wavelength and momentum are inversely related","D":"The wavelength depends only on the particle's charge under standard test conditions"},"correct":"C"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-phys-3.12"},{"id":"PHYS-E3B-02","spec":"PHYS-3","stem":"A geostationary satellite must orbit:","options":{"A":"over either pole with a one-hour period","B":"above the equator with a 24-hour period","C":"in any direction with zero speed","D":"below the atmosphere"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GPHYS-FIELDS-ELECTROMAGNETISM","scaffold":"Its orbital period and direction match Earth's rotation so it remains above one longitude.","reforge":{"stem":"Which orbital properties must a satellite have to stay above the same point on Earth's surface at all times?","options":{"A":"An orbit above the equator with a period of 24 hours","B":"An orbit over the poles with a period of one hour under standard test conditions","C":"Any orbit at all, provided its speed is zero","D":"An orbit below the atmosphere"},"correct":"A"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-phys-3.9"},{"id":"PHYS-E3B-03","spec":"PHYS-3","stem":"The intensity of a wave is proportional to:","options":{"A":"its wavelength only","B":"the reciprocal of frequency always","C":"the square of its amplitude","D":"its phase difference only"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GPHYS-WAVES","scaffold":"For many waves, doubling amplitude gives four times the intensity.","reforge":{"stem":"If a wave's amplitude is doubled, what happens to its intensity?","options":{"A":"It stays the same, since intensity depends only on wavelength under standard test conditions","B":"It quadruples, since intensity is proportional to amplitude squared","C":"It halves, since intensity is the reciprocal of frequency","D":"It depends only on the wave's phase difference"},"correct":"B"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-phys-3.3"},{"id":"PHYS-E3B-04","spec":"PHYS-3","stem":"The uncertainty in a measured quantity is reduced by:","options":{"A":"rounding to fewer figures","B":"A conclusion where variables were changed together under standard test conditions","C":"discarding consistent results","D":"using suitable resolution and repeating measurements"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GPHYS-MEASUREMENT","scaffold":"Repeats help estimate random uncertainty; instrument resolution limits precision.","reforge":{"stem":"Which practical steps most directly reduce the uncertainty in an experimental result?","options":{"A":"Rounding the final answer to fewer significant figures under standard test conditions","B":"Changing the units without taking a new reading","C":"Using an instrument with suitable resolution and repeating the measurement","D":"Discarding results that are consistent with each other"},"correct":"C"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-phys-3.1"},{"id":"PHYS-E3B-05","spec":"PHYS-3","stem":"A red-shift in a galaxy's spectrum indicates that its light has:","options":{"A":"shifted towards longer wavelengths as it recedes","B":"When the wire is placed outside the field entirely under standard conditions","C":"lost all frequency","D":"become independent of motion"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GPHYS-FIELDS-ELECTROMAGNETISM","scaffold":"Cosmological red-shift provides evidence of expansion when interpreted with other evidence.","reforge":{"stem":"A distant galaxy's spectral lines are found shifted towards the red end of the spectrum. What does this indicate about the galaxy's motion?","options":{"A":"The galaxy is moving away, shifting its light to longer wavelengths","B":"The galaxy is approaching, shifting its light to shorter wavelengths","C":"The galaxy's light has lost all frequency","D":"The galaxy's motion has no effect on its spectrum"},"correct":"A"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-phys-3.9"},{"id":"PHYS-E3B-06","spec":"PHYS-3","stem":"The critical angle is the angle of incidence for which the refracted ray is:","options":{"A":"returned along the normal","B":"along the boundary at 90 degrees","C":"absorbed completely","D":"When the wire is parallel to the magnetic field"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GPHYS-FIELDS-ELECTROMAGNETISM","scaffold":"Beyond the critical angle, total internal reflection can occur in the denser medium.","reforge":{"stem":"As the angle of incidence in a denser medium increases toward the critical angle, what happens to the refracted ray just before total internal reflection begins?","options":{"A":"It is reflected straight back along the normal under standard test conditions in a typical experiment","B":"It travels along the boundary between the two media, at 90 degrees to the normal","C":"It is completely absorbed at the boundary","D":"It travels parallel to the incident ray"},"correct":"B"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-phys-3.3"},{"id":"PHYS-GAPS-01","spec":"PHYS-3","stem":"Medical ultrasound imaging relies mainly on:","options":{"A":"visible light absorbed by bone","B":"gamma rays from a radioactive source","C":"reflected high-frequency sound waves","D":"direct current through tissue"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GPHYS-WAVES","scaffold":"Echoes from boundaries between tissues can be used to build an image.","reforge":{"stem":"Why can ultrasound scanning produce an image of internal organs without ionising radiation?","options":{"A":"Because visible light is absorbed differently by bone","B":"Because gamma rays are emitted from a radioactive source under standard test conditions","C":"Because reflected high-frequency sound waves are detected at tissue boundaries","D":"Because direct current is passed through the tissue"},"correct":"C"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-phys-3.10"},{"id":"PHYS-GAPS-02","spec":"PHYS-3","stem":"MRI produces images by detecting signals from nuclei in:","options":{"A":"a beam of alpha particles","B":"Its apparent angular size as seen from Earth under standard test conditions","C":"heated electrical wires","D":"a strong magnetic field after radio-frequency excitation"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GPHYS-ASTRONOMY","scaffold":"Hydrogen nuclei respond to the magnetic field and radio-frequency pulses.","reforge":{"stem":"What physical principle allows an MRI scanner to produce detailed images of soft tissue?","options":{"A":"Nuclei responding to a strong magnetic field after radio-frequency excitation","B":"A beam of alpha particles passing through the body under standard test conditions","C":"Ultraviolet fluorescence emitted by the tissue","D":"Heated electrical wires placed near the tissue"},"correct":"A"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-phys-3.10"},{"id":"PHYS-GAPS-03","spec":"PHYS-3","stem":"Radiotherapy is designed to treat tumours by:","options":{"A":"damaging cancer-cell DNA with ionising radiation","B":"Intensity determines how fast electrons move through the metal.","C":"increasing the tumour's blood glucose","D":"cooling healthy tissue to absolute zero"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GPHYS-QUANTUM-NUCLEAR","scaffold":"Treatment aims to maximise damage to tumour cells while limiting harm to healthy tissue.","reforge":{"stem":"How does radiotherapy work to destroy cancerous cells while trying to spare healthy tissue?","options":{"A":"Ultrasound is used to measure the patient's blood pressure under standard test conditions","B":"Ionising radiation damages the DNA of the targeted cancer cells","C":"The tumour's blood glucose level is deliberately increased","D":"Healthy tissue is cooled to absolute zero"},"correct":"B"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-phys-3.10"},{"id":"PHYS-GAPS-04","spec":"PHYS-3","stem":"In engineering physics, a safety factor is used to:","options":{"A":"make a structure carry no load","B":"allow for uncertainty between expected and maximum loading","C":"It is unable to store any elastic energy under standard test conditions","D":"remove the need to test materials"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GPHYS-SPRINGS-MATERIALS","scaffold":"Designers compare failure load with working load to manage uncertainty.","reforge":{"stem":"Why do engineers design a bridge to withstand far more load than it is ever expected to carry?","options":{"A":"To ensure the structure carries no load at all under standard test conditions in a typical experiment","B":"To guarantee that fatigue can never occur","C":"To build in a safety factor allowing for uncertainty between expected and maximum load","D":"To avoid the need for any material testing"},"correct":"C"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-phys-3.11"},{"id":"PHYS-GAPS-05","spec":"PHYS-3","stem":"A material with a large Young modulus is generally:","options":{"A":"unable to store elastic energy","B":"very soft and easily stretched","C":"stiff, so it needs a large stress for a given strain","D":"The spring has already passed its elastic limit under standard conditions"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GPHYS-SPRINGS-MATERIALS","scaffold":"Young modulus is the ratio of tensile stress to tensile strain in the elastic region.","reforge":{"stem":"A material needs a very large stress to produce even a small strain. What does this tell you about its Young modulus?","options":{"A":"It has a large Young modulus, meaning it is stiff","B":"It is unable to store any elastic energy under standard test conditions","C":"It is very soft and stretches easily","D":"It must always be brittle under any load"},"correct":"A"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-phys-3.4"},{"id":"PHYS-GAPS-06","spec":"PHYS-3","stem":"A NAND gate produces a false output only when:","options":{"A":"both inputs are false","B":"one input is disconnected","C":"the inputs are different","D":"both inputs are true"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GPHYS-CIRCUITS","scaffold":"NAND is the inverse of AND and is false only for two true inputs.","reforge":{"stem":"A logic gate outputs false only when both of its inputs are true, and true in every other case. Which gate is this?","options":{"A":"A gate that outputs false when both inputs are false","B":"A NAND gate","C":"A gate affected by a disconnected input","D":"A gate that outputs false only when the inputs differ"},"correct":"B"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-phys-3.3"},{"id":"PHYS-GAPS-07","spec":"PHYS-3","stem":"A transistor can be used as a switch when it is operated between:","options":{"A":"cut-off and saturation","B":"the alpha and beta thresholds","C":"two states of nuclear decay","D":"the positive and negative terminals of a transformer"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GPHYS-CIRCUITS","scaffold":"Cut-off represents no collector current and saturation represents a conducting state.","reforge":{"stem":"When a transistor is used purely as an electronic switch, between which two states is it operated?","options":{"A":"The alpha and beta decay thresholds","B":"Two different states of nuclear decay","C":"Cut-off and saturation","D":"The positive and negative terminals of a transformer"},"correct":"C"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-phys-3.13"},{"id":"PHYS-GAPS-08","spec":"PHYS-3","stem":"A diode is commonly used to:","options":{"A":"increase resistance equally in both directions","B":"allow current mainly in one direction","C":"convert every alternating signal to sound","D":"store magnetic flux permanently"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GPHYS-CIRCUITS","scaffold":"Its asymmetric current-voltage characteristic supports rectification.","reforge":{"stem":"Why is a diode included in a circuit that must only allow current to flow one way?","options":{"A":"A diode allows current to pass mainly in one direction","B":"A diode increases resistance equally in both directions","C":"A diode converts every alternating signal into sound","D":"A diode stores magnetic flux permanently"},"correct":"A"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-phys-3.13"},{"id":"PHYS-GAPS-09","spec":"PHYS-3","stem":"A logic circuit's truth table shows:","options":{"A":"e.m.f. always exceeds terminal p.d. by exactly 0.5 V.","B":"the resistance of each wire only","C":"the output for every possible combination of inputs","D":"the speed of a nuclear reaction"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GPHYS-CIRCUITS","scaffold":"Truth tables describe logical behaviour independently of the circuit layout.","reforge":{"stem":"What information does a logic circuit's truth table provide?","options":{"A":"The physical size of the circuit","B":"The output produced for every possible combination of inputs","C":"The resistance of each individual wire under standard test conditions","D":"The speed of a nuclear reaction"},"correct":"B"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-phys-3.13"},{"id":"PHYS-MEASURE-01","spec":"PHYS-3","stem":"Random uncertainty can be reduced most effectively by:","options":{"A":"removing every value that differs","B":"A conclusion based on a single anomalous result","C":"recording only the first result","D":"repeating readings and calculating a mean"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GPHYS-MEASUREMENT","scaffold":"Repeats reveal random variation and allow a more reliable mean.","reforge":{"stem":"A student takes many repeated timings of the same event and calculates their mean. Why does this reduce random uncertainty?","options":{"A":"Changing the unit after each reading removes uncertainty","B":"Recording only the first result removes uncertainty","C":"Repeating readings and averaging cancels out random scatter in the data","D":"Removing every value that differs from the rest removes uncertainty under standard test conditions"},"correct":"C"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-phys-3.1"},{"id":"PHYS-MEASURE-02","spec":"PHYS-3","stem":"A systematic error is suggested when repeated readings are:","options":{"A":"consistently offset in the same direction","B":"spread randomly around a mean","C":"A conclusion drawn without recording any units","D":"recorded with different units"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GPHYS-MEASUREMENT","scaffold":"A systematic error shifts measurements consistently and is not removed by averaging.","reforge":{"stem":"Every reading in an experiment comes out a fixed amount higher than the true value, in the same direction each time. What does this suggest?","options":{"A":"A systematic error, since the readings are consistently offset","B":"A random error, since the readings are scattered around a mean","C":"A problem caused by measuring different variables","D":"A problem caused by recording different units"},"correct":"A"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-phys-3.1"},{"id":"PHYS-MEASURE-03","spec":"PHYS-3","stem":"The resolution of a measuring instrument is:","options":{"A":"Percentage uncertainty in the instrument","B":"the smallest change it can distinguish","C":"the average of all readings","D":"the percentage error in a result"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GPHYS-MEASUREMENT","scaffold":"Resolution describes the smallest scale division or change that can be read.","reforge":{"stem":"What does the resolution of a measuring instrument actually tell you?","options":{"A":"The largest value the instrument is able to display under standard test conditions","B":"The smallest change in a quantity that the instrument can detect","C":"The average of every reading taken","D":"The percentage error in a single result"},"correct":"B"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-phys-3.1"},{"id":"PHYS-MEASURE-04","spec":"PHYS-3","stem":"Percentage uncertainty is found by:","options":{"A":"measured value divided by absolute uncertainty","B":"adding the units to the reading","C":"absolute uncertainty divided by measured value, times 100","D":"A random error, since the readings are scattered around a mean"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GPHYS-MEASUREMENT","scaffold":"The ratio compares the uncertainty with the size of the measured quantity.","reforge":{"stem":"How is the percentage uncertainty in a measurement calculated?","options":{"A":"By dividing the measured value by the absolute uncertainty under standard test conditions","B":"By adding the unit onto the raw reading","C":"By dividing the absolute uncertainty by the measured value and multiplying by 100","D":"By subtracting the smallest reading from the largest"},"correct":"C"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-phys-3.1"},{"id":"PHYS-MEASURE-05","spec":"PHYS-3","stem":"Why should a measurement be recorded with an appropriate number of significant figures?","options":{"A":"The largest value the instrument is able to display under standard test conditions","B":"More digits always remove systematic error","C":"Fewer digits guarantee a valid result","D":"Extra digits can imply precision that the apparatus does not provide"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GPHYS-MEASUREMENT","scaffold":"Reported precision should reflect the instrument and uncertainty.","reforge":{"stem":"Why is it misleading to record a measurement with more significant figures than the apparatus can actually justify?","options":{"A":"Extra digits can suggest a level of precision the apparatus does not really provide","B":"Significant figures remove the need for repeated readings under standard test conditions","C":"More digits always eliminate systematic error","D":"Fewer digits automatically guarantee a valid result"},"correct":"A"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-phys-3.1"},{"id":"PHYS-MEASURE-06","spec":"PHYS-3","stem":"A calibration error can be identified by:","options":{"A":"checking the instrument against a known standard","B":"Discarding results that are consistent with each other","C":"changing the dependent variable","D":"rounding every result to one figure"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GPHYS-MEASUREMENT","scaffold":"Comparison with a standard can reveal a consistent offset in the scale or zero.","reforge":{"stem":"How can a student check whether a measuring instrument has a calibration error?","options":{"A":"By repeating the same uncalibrated reading again","B":"By comparing its reading against a known, trusted standard","C":"By changing the dependent variable being measured","D":"By rounding every result to one significant figure under standard test conditions"},"correct":"B"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-phys-3.1"},{"id":"PHYS-MEASURE-07","spec":"PHYS-3","stem":"Which result is most precise?","options":{"A":"A single reading near the accepted value","B":"Several readings close together","C":"A result with no unit","D":"Readings spread over a wide range"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GPHYS-MEASUREMENT","scaffold":"Precision concerns the agreement between repeated readings.","reforge":{"stem":"Which set of repeated results shows the greatest precision?","options":{"A":"A single reading that happens to be near the accepted value","B":"A result recorded without any unit","C":"Several readings that are all closely grouped together","D":"Readings that are spread over a wide range"},"correct":"C"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-phys-3.3"},{"id":"PHYS-MEASURE-08","spec":"PHYS-3","stem":"Which result is most accurate?","options":{"A":"A set of values with a large spread","B":"Changing the units without taking a new reading","C":"A measurement close to the accepted value","D":"A result recorded without uncertainty"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GPHYS-MEASUREMENT","scaffold":"Accuracy concerns closeness to the accepted or true value.","reforge":{"stem":"Which single result would be described as the most accurate?","options":{"A":"A measurement that lies close to the accepted true value","B":"A set of values with a very large spread","C":"A reading quoted with many unsupported digits","D":"A result recorded without any stated uncertainty under standard test conditions"},"correct":"A"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-phys-3.3"},{"id":"PHYS-MEASURE-09","spec":"PHYS-3","stem":"A student repeats a timing experiment and obtains 2.41, 2.42 and 2.40 s. The readings are:","options":{"A":"systematically wrong by definition","B":"accurate only because three readings were taken","C":"invalid because they are not identical","D":"precise because they are closely grouped"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GPHYS-MEASUREMENT","scaffold":"The close grouping indicates good precision; accuracy needs comparison with an accepted value.","reforge":{"stem":"A student repeats a timing experiment and obtains 5.02, 5.03 and 5.01 s. What can be said about these readings?","options":{"A":"They are systematically wrong by definition","B":"They are precise, because they are closely grouped together","C":"They are accurate simply because three readings were taken under standard test conditions","D":"They are invalid because they are not identical"},"correct":"B"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-phys-3.1"},{"id":"PHYS-MEASURE-10","spec":"PHYS-3","stem":"When adding measured quantities, absolute uncertainties are usually:","options":{"A":"combined by addition","B":"multiplied by the number of units","C":"ignored if the values are large","D":"converted into standard deviation automatically"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GPHYS-MEASUREMENT","scaffold":"For sums and differences, absolute uncertainties are added as a conservative estimate.","reforge":{"stem":"When two measured lengths are added together to find a total length, how should their absolute uncertainties be combined?","options":{"A":"The absolute uncertainties should be multiplied by the number of quantities","B":"The absolute uncertainties should be ignored if the values are large","C":"The absolute uncertainties should be added together","D":"The absolute uncertainties should be automatically converted into a standard deviation"},"correct":"C"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-phys-3.3"},{"id":"PHYS-N3-01","stem":"The photoelectric effect provides evidence that light behaves as:","options":{"A":"A continuous wave and nothing else","B":"Discrete packets called photons","C":"A stream of electrons","D":"A longitudinal wave"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GPHYS-QUANTUM-NUCLEAR","scaffold":"Wave theory predicts that any frequency of light should eventually eject electrons if intense enough, and that emission should be delayed at low intensity. Neither happens. Emission is instantaneous and occurs only above a threshold frequency, whatever the intensity — explained if light arrives as photons of energy E = hf, each interacting with one electron. Intensity then sets the number of photons, not the energy of each.","reforge":{"stem":"Below the threshold frequency, increasing the intensity of the light causes:","options":{"A":"More electrons to be emitted","B":"Electrons to be emitted with less energy","C":"Electrons to be emitted more slowly","D":"No electrons to be emitted at all"},"correct":"D"},"spec":"PHYS-3","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-phys-3.12"},{"id":"PHYS-N3-02","stem":"In the photoelectric equation hf = φ + Ek(max), the term φ represents the:","options":{"A":"Photon momentum","B":"Maximum kinetic energy of electrons","C":"Work function of the metal","D":"Frequency of the light"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GPHYS-QUANTUM-NUCLEAR","scaffold":"The work function φ is the minimum energy needed to remove an electron from the metal surface, so the threshold frequency satisfies hf₀ = φ. Any photon energy above that appears as kinetic energy of the emitted electron, with the maximum given by hf − φ. Plotting Ek(max) against f gives a straight line of gradient h and intercept −φ, which is how the Planck constant is measured.","reforge":{"stem":"A graph of maximum kinetic energy against frequency for the photoelectric effect has gradient equal to:","options":{"A":"The Planck constant","B":"The work function","C":"The threshold frequency","D":"The electron charge"},"correct":"A"},"spec":"PHYS-3","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-phys-3.3"},{"id":"PHYS-N3-03","stem":"A photon has frequency 5.0 × 10¹⁴ Hz. Using h = 6.63 × 10⁻³⁴ J s, its energy is:","options":{"A":"6.6 × 10⁻³⁴ J","B":"1.3 × 10⁻⁴⁸ J","C":"7.5 × 10⁴⁷ J","D":"3.3 × 10⁻¹⁹ J"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GPHYS-QUANTUM-NUCLEAR","scaffold":"Photon energy E = hf = 6.63 × 10⁻³⁴ × 5.0 × 10¹⁴ = 3.3 × 10⁻¹⁹ J. Where wavelength is given instead, use E = hc/λ. Because these energies are tiny, the electronvolt is often used: 1 eV = 1.60 × 10⁻¹⁹ J, so this photon carries about 2.1 eV. Dividing rather than multiplying by h is the usual arithmetic slip.","reforge":{"stem":"An energy of 3.2 × 10⁻¹⁹ J expressed in electronvolts is approximately:","options":{"A":"3.2 eV","B":"2.0 eV","C":"1.6 eV","D":"0.5 eV"},"correct":"B"},"spec":"PHYS-3","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-phys-3.4"},{"id":"PHYS-N3-04","stem":"The de Broglie wavelength of a particle is given by:","options":{"A":"λ = h/p","B":"λ = hp","C":"λ = p/h","D":"λ = hf"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GPHYS-QUANTUM-NUCLEAR","scaffold":"De Broglie proposed λ = h/p = h/mv, so every particle has an associated wavelength. Because h is so small, the wavelength is negligible for everyday objects but comparable to atomic spacing for electrons, which is why electron diffraction through a thin graphite film is observable. That experiment is the direct evidence for wave–particle duality in matter.","reforge":{"stem":"Electron diffraction provides evidence that particles can behave as:","options":{"A":"Charged spheres only","B":"Photons","C":"Waves","D":"Nuclei"},"correct":"C"},"spec":"PHYS-3","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-phys-3.12"},{"id":"PHYS-N3-05","stem":"An electron in an atom moves from a higher to a lower energy level. The result is:","options":{"A":"Absorption of a photon","B":"Emission of a photon","C":"Emission of an alpha particle","D":"An increase in the mass of the atom"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GPHYS-QUANTUM-NUCLEAR","scaffold":"Energy levels in an atom are discrete, so transitions emit or absorb photons of precisely defined energy: hf = E₁ − E₂. Downward transitions emit, upward transitions absorb. This produces line spectra rather than continuous ones — emission lines where a hot gas radiates, and dark absorption lines where a cool gas absorbs from a continuous background, as in the Sun's spectrum.","reforge":{"stem":"Line spectra rather than continuous spectra are evidence that atomic energy levels are:","options":{"A":"Identical in all elements","B":"Continuous","C":"Always negative","D":"Discrete"},"correct":"D"},"spec":"PHYS-3","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-phys-3.4"},{"id":"PHYS-N3-06","stem":"In fluorescence, an atom absorbs an ultraviolet photon and emits visible light because it:","options":{"A":"Emits a single photon of higher energy instead","B":"Splits into two atoms","C":"Emits lower-energy photons in stages","D":"Loses an electron permanently"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GPHYS-QUANTUM-NUCLEAR","scaffold":"Absorbing a high-energy ultraviolet photon excites an electron to a high level. It then returns in several smaller steps, each emitting a photon of lower energy and so longer wavelength, some in the visible range. Energy is conserved: the emitted photons' energies sum to no more than the absorbed one. This is how fluorescent tubes convert ultraviolet from a mercury discharge into visible light.","reforge":{"stem":"The photons emitted during fluorescence have a wavelength that is:","options":{"A":"Longer than the absorbed photon","B":"Shorter than the absorbed photon","C":"The same as the absorbed photon","D":"Unrelated to the absorbed photon"},"correct":"A"},"spec":"PHYS-3","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-phys-3.2"},{"id":"PHYS-N3-07","stem":"An alpha particle consists of:","options":{"A":"A proton only","B":"A single electron","C":"A single high-energy photon","D":"Two protons, two neutrons"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GPHYS-QUANTUM-NUCLEAR","scaffold":"Alpha is a helium nucleus: 2 protons and 2 neutrons, charge +2e, strongly ionising but stopped by paper or a few centimetres of air. Beta-minus is a fast electron from a neutron decaying, stopped by a few millimetres of aluminium. Gamma is a high-energy photon with no charge or mass, reduced but never fully stopped by lead. Ionising power and penetrating power run in opposite orders.","reforge":{"stem":"Which type of radiation is the most penetrating but the least ionising?","options":{"A":"Alpha","B":"Gamma","C":"Beta","D":"All are equal"},"correct":"B"},"spec":"PHYS-3","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-phys-3.2"},{"id":"PHYS-N3-08","stem":"In beta-minus decay, a neutron in the nucleus changes into a:","options":{"A":"Proton, electron, antineutrino","B":"Proton only","C":"A positron together with a neutrino","D":"Alpha particle"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GPHYS-QUANTUM-NUCLEAR","scaffold":"Beta-minus decay: n → p + e⁻ + antineutrino. The proton number rises by one and the nucleon number is unchanged. Beta-plus decay is the reverse: p → n + e⁺ + neutrino, lowering proton number. The neutrino was postulated because the emitted electrons have a range of energies rather than a single value, which would otherwise breach conservation of energy and momentum.","reforge":{"stem":"The existence of the neutrino was proposed to account for the fact that beta particles are emitted with:","options":{"A":"One single fixed energy value","B":"Negative mass","C":"A continuous range","D":"No energy at all"},"correct":"C"},"spec":"PHYS-3","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-phys-3.3"},{"id":"PHYS-N3-09","stem":"A nuclide has 92 protons and 146 neutrons. Its nucleon number is:","options":{"A":"92","B":"238","C":"146","D":"54"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GPHYS-QUANTUM-NUCLEAR","scaffold":"Nucleon number A = protons + neutrons = 92 + 146 = 238; proton number Z = 92 identifies the element as uranium. Isotopes share Z but differ in A. In nuclear equations both A and Z must balance on each side, which is how the identity of a decay product is deduced — alpha emission reduces A by 4 and Z by 2.","reforge":{"stem":"A nucleus emits an alpha particle. Its nucleon number decreases by:","options":{"A":"0","B":"2","C":"1","D":"4"},"correct":"D"},"spec":"PHYS-3","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-phys-3.3"},{"id":"PHYS-N3-10","stem":"The half-life of a radioactive isotope is the time taken for:","options":{"A":"All the nuclei to decay","B":"The mass of the sample to halve","C":"Half the remaining nuclei","D":"The activity to fall to zero"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GPHYS-QUANTUM-NUCLEAR","scaffold":"Half-life is the time for half the remaining undecayed nuclei to decay, and is constant for a given isotope regardless of how much is present, because decay is a random process with a fixed probability per nucleus per unit time. Activity A = λN falls exponentially, so after n half-lives the fraction remaining is (½)ⁿ. Radioactive decay is unaffected by temperature, pressure or chemical state.","reforge":{"stem":"After 3 half-lives, the fraction of the original nuclei remaining is:","options":{"A":"1/8","B":"1/3","C":"1/6","D":"1/9"},"correct":"A"},"spec":"PHYS-3","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-phys-3.8"},{"id":"PHYS-N3-11","stem":"A sample has activity 800 Bq and a half-life of 5 days. Its activity after 15 days is:","options":{"A":"0 Bq","B":"400 Bq","C":"267 Bq","D":"100 Bq"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GPHYS-QUANTUM-NUCLEAR","scaffold":"15 days is three half-lives, so the activity halves three times: 800 → 400 → 200 → 100 Bq. Equivalently A = A₀(½)³ = 800/8. The becquerel is one decay per second. Because decay is exponential, the activity approaches but never reaches zero, so answers of exactly zero after a finite time are always wrong.","reforge":{"stem":"The activity of a source is related to the number of undecayed nuclei by:","options":{"A":"A = λ/N","B":"A = λN","C":"A = N/λ","D":"A = λN²"},"correct":"B"},"spec":"PHYS-3","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-phys-3.8"},{"id":"PHYS-N3-12","stem":"The binding energy of a nucleus is the energy required to:","options":{"A":"Separate it into free nucleons","B":"Remove a single electron from the atom","C":"Cause the nucleus to fission","D":"Ionise the atom completely"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GPHYS-QUANTUM-NUCLEAR","scaffold":"Binding energy is the work needed to pull a nucleus apart into free nucleons, and appears as the mass defect: the nucleus is lighter than its separate nucleons, with ΔE = Δmc². Binding energy per nucleon peaks near iron-56, which is why fusing light nuclei and splitting heavy ones both release energy — each moves the products towards that peak.","reforge":{"stem":"Energy is released in both fusion of light nuclei and fission of heavy nuclei because both increase the:","options":{"A":"Total number of nucleons present","B":"Proton number","C":"Binding energy per nucleon","D":"Mass of the products"},"correct":"C"},"spec":"PHYS-3","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-phys-3.8"},{"id":"PHYS-N3-13","stem":"In nuclear fission, a heavy nucleus is typically induced to split by absorbing a:","options":{"A":"Proton","B":"Neutron","C":"Gamma photon","D":"Beta particle"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GPHYS-QUANTUM-NUCLEAR","scaffold":"A slow (thermal) neutron is absorbed by uranium-235, making it unstable so that it splits into two smaller nuclei plus two or three fast neutrons and considerable energy. Those neutrons can induce further fissions — a chain reaction. Neutrons are used because they are uncharged and so are not repelled by the nucleus, unlike protons or alpha particles.","reforge":{"stem":"In a nuclear reactor, the moderator's function is to:","options":{"A":"Shield the operators","B":"Absorb excess neutrons","C":"Cool the reactor core","D":"Slow the neutrons down"},"correct":"D"},"spec":"PHYS-3","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-phys-3.8"},{"id":"PHYS-N3-14","stem":"Control rods in a nuclear reactor work by:","options":{"A":"Slowing neutrons to thermal speeds","B":"Reflecting neutrons back into the fuel rods","C":"Absorbing neutrons to control rate","D":"Transferring heat to the coolant"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GPHYS-QUANTUM-NUCLEAR","scaffold":"Control rods of boron or cadmium absorb neutrons, and raising or lowering them adjusts how many are available to cause further fission, keeping the chain reaction critical and steady. The moderator, often graphite or water, slows neutrons so uranium-235 absorbs them efficiently — a distinct role. The coolant removes heat, and thick concrete and lead shielding protects against radiation.","reforge":{"stem":"A chain reaction is described as critical when each fission causes, on average:","options":{"A":"Exactly one further fission","B":"No further fissions","C":"Two further fissions","D":"An increasing number of fissions"},"correct":"A"},"spec":"PHYS-3","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-phys-3.8"},{"id":"PHYS-N3-15","stem":"Nuclear fusion requires extremely high temperatures in order to:","options":{"A":"Slow the nuclei down","B":"Melt the nuclei together","C":"Ionise the air surrounding the reactor","D":"Overcome electrostatic repulsion"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GPHYS-QUANTUM-NUCLEAR","scaffold":"Nuclei are positively charged, so they repel; fusing them requires enough kinetic energy to bring them within range of the strong nuclear force, which acts only over about 10⁻¹⁵ m. That means temperatures of millions of kelvin, plus high density and sufficient confinement time. Achieving all three simultaneously on Earth is the central engineering difficulty of fusion power.","reforge":{"stem":"The strong nuclear force differs from the electrostatic force in that it is:","options":{"A":"Weaker than gravity","B":"Effective over short ranges","C":"Always repulsive","D":"Acting only upon orbital electrons"},"correct":"B"},"spec":"PHYS-3","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-phys-3.8"},{"id":"PHYS-N3-16","stem":"Rutherford's alpha scattering experiment showed that the atom's positive charge is:","options":{"A":"Concentrated in a tiny dense nucleus","B":"Spread evenly throughout the whole atom","C":"Carried by the electrons","D":"Absent altogether"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GPHYS-QUANTUM-NUCLEAR","scaffold":"Most alpha particles passed straight through the gold foil, showing the atom is mostly empty space. A small fraction deflected through large angles, and a very few came almost straight back, which requires a concentrated positive charge of large mass — the nucleus. This disproved the plum pudding model, in which charge was spread evenly and only small deflections would be expected.","reforge":{"stem":"That most alpha particles passed straight through the gold foil showed the atom is:","options":{"A":"Uniformly solid","B":"Made only of electrons","C":"Mostly empty space","D":"Negatively charged overall"},"correct":"C"},"spec":"PHYS-3","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-phys-3.3"},{"id":"PHYS-N3-17","stem":"Which particle is a lepton?","options":{"A":"Proton","B":"Electron","C":"Neutrons","D":"Pion"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GPHYS-QUANTUM-NUCLEAR","scaffold":"Leptons — electrons, muons, taus and their neutrinos — are fundamental and do not feel the strong force. Hadrons feel the strong force and are made of quarks: baryons contain three quarks (protons, neutrons), while mesons contain a quark and an antiquark (pions, kaons). Lepton number and baryon number are separately conserved in every interaction, which constrains which decays are possible.","reforge":{"stem":"A baryon such as a proton is composed of:","options":{"A":"No quarks at all","B":"Two quarks","C":"A quark and an antiquark","D":"Three quarks"},"correct":"D"},"spec":"PHYS-3","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-phys-3.2"},{"id":"PHYS-N3-18","stem":"A proton is composed of the quark combination:","options":{"A":"ddd","B":"udd","C":"uud","D":"uuu"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GPHYS-QUANTUM-NUCLEAR","scaffold":"An up quark has charge +2/3 and a down quark −1/3. A proton is uud: 2/3 + 2/3 − 1/3 = +1. A neutron is udd: 2/3 − 1/3 − 1/3 = 0. Checking that the charges sum correctly is the quickest way to confirm a quark composition. In beta-minus decay a down quark changes into an up quark, which is what converts a neutron into a proton.","reforge":{"stem":"In beta-minus decay, the quark change that occurs is:","options":{"A":"A down quark becomes an up quark","B":"An up quark becomes a down quark","C":"A quark is destroyed","D":"Two quarks combine"},"correct":"A"},"spec":"PHYS-3","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-phys-3.2"},{"id":"PHYS-N3-19","stem":"When a particle meets its antiparticle, the result is:","options":{"A":"Emission of a neutron","B":"A single larger particle forming","C":"No interaction at all","D":"Annihilation, producing photons"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GPHYS-QUANTUM-NUCLEAR","scaffold":"Annihilation converts the entire mass of both particles into energy as two photons, each of at least E = mc² for one particle, with two produced to conserve momentum. Pair production is the reverse: a photon of sufficient energy creates a particle–antiparticle pair, requiring a minimum photon energy of 2mc² and the presence of a nucleus to conserve momentum. PET scanning uses annihilation of positrons.","reforge":{"stem":"Pair production requires a photon with a minimum energy equal to:","options":{"A":"mc²","B":"2mc²","C":"½mc²","D":"hf/2"},"correct":"B"},"spec":"PHYS-3","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-phys-3.2"},{"id":"PHYS-N3-20","stem":"An X-ray tube produces X-rays when fast electrons:","options":{"A":"Decelerate on striking the anode","B":"Are absorbed by the cathode","C":"Are emitted directly from a radioactive source","D":"Travel through a magnetic field"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GPHYS-QUANTUM-NUCLEAR","scaffold":"Electrons accelerated through a high pd strike a heavy metal target and decelerate sharply, radiating a continuous X-ray spectrum (bremsstrahlung), with characteristic sharp lines superimposed from inner-shell electron transitions in the target atoms. The maximum X-ray photon energy equals the electron's kinetic energy, eV, so the minimum wavelength is set by the accelerating voltage.","reforge":{"stem":"Increasing the accelerating voltage in an X-ray tube changes the minimum X-ray wavelength produced by making it:","options":{"A":"Longer","B":"Zero","C":"Shorter","D":"Unchanged"},"correct":"C"},"spec":"PHYS-3","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-phys-3.10"},{"id":"PHYS-N3-21","stem":"The intensity of gamma radiation passing through an absorber falls:","options":{"A":"Linearly with thickness","B":"Exponentially with thickness","C":"To zero at a definite range","D":"Only if the absorber is charged"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GPHYS-QUANTUM-NUCLEAR","scaffold":"Gamma intensity follows I = I₀e^(−μx), an exponential decay, so it is reduced but never entirely stopped — there is no definite range, unlike alpha and beta which have finite ranges. The half-value thickness is the depth halving the intensity. Alpha and beta lose energy through repeated ionising collisions and so stop abruptly, which is why their absorption curves look quite different.","reforge":{"stem":"Unlike gamma radiation, alpha particles in a material have:","options":{"A":"Increasing energy with depth","B":"An exponential absorption curve","C":"No interaction with matter","D":"A definite, finite range"},"correct":"D"},"spec":"PHYS-3","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-phys-3.2"},{"id":"PHYS-N3-22","stem":"Background radiation must be subtracted from readings because it comes from:","options":{"A":"The detector's own internal circuitry","B":"Cosmic rays alone","C":"Natural and artificial sources","D":"The sample being measured"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GPHYS-QUANTUM-NUCLEAR","scaffold":"Background radiation comes from radon gas, rocks and soil, cosmic rays, food and drink, and artificial sources chiefly medical. It is always present, so the background count rate must be measured separately and subtracted to obtain the true count from a source. Because decay is random, counts fluctuate, so longer counting times give more reliable averages.","reforge":{"stem":"Radioactive decay is described as a random process, which means the:","options":{"A":"When a nucleus decays is unpredictable","B":"Half-life of the isotope changes unpredictably","C":"Activity increases over time","D":"Decay depends on temperature"},"correct":"A"},"spec":"PHYS-3","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-phys-3.2"},{"id":"PHYS-N3-23","stem":"A capacitor of 100 μF is charged to 12 V. The charge stored is:","options":{"A":"12 C","B":"8.3 C","C":"1200 coulombs","D":"1.2 × 10⁻³ C"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GPHYS-CIRCUITS","scaffold":"Q = CV = 100 × 10⁻⁶ × 12 = 1.2 × 10⁻³ C, or 1.2 mC. The micro prefix means 10⁻⁶, and failing to convert is the usual error, giving an answer a million times too large. The energy stored is W = ½QV = ½ × 1.2 × 10⁻³ × 12 = 7.2 × 10⁻³ J, or equivalently ½CV².","reforge":{"stem":"The energy stored by a 100 μF capacitor charged to 12 V is:","options":{"A":"1.2 × 10⁻³ J","B":"7.2 × 10⁻³ J","C":"14.4 J","D":"0.6 J"},"correct":"B"},"spec":"PHYS-3","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-phys-3.5"},{"id":"PHYS-N3-24","stem":"Two capacitors are connected in parallel. The total capacitance is:","options":{"A":"The sum of the capacitances","B":"Found by adding the reciprocals","C":"Always smaller than either","D":"The product of the two"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GPHYS-CIRCUITS","scaffold":"Capacitors behave opposite to resistors: in parallel capacitances add, since the effective plate area increases; in series the reciprocals add, giving a total smaller than the smallest. Series capacitors share the same charge while the pd divides; parallel capacitors share the same pd while the charge divides. Confusing these with the resistor rules is the standard error.","reforge":{"stem":"Two identical capacitors are connected in series. The total capacitance is:","options":{"A":"Double one capacitor","B":"Zero","C":"Half one capacitor","D":"Equal to one capacitor"},"correct":"C"},"spec":"PHYS-3","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-phys-3.5"},{"id":"PHYS-N3-25","stem":"A wave is described as electromagnetic. This means it:","options":{"A":"Requires a material medium to travel through","B":"Oscillating electric and magnetic fields","C":"Is longitudinal in nature","D":"Travels at the speed of sound"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GPHYS-WAVES","scaffold":"Electromagnetic waves are transverse oscillations of perpendicular electric and magnetic fields, requiring no medium and travelling at 3.00 × 10⁸ m s⁻¹ in a vacuum. The spectrum runs from radio (longest wavelength, lowest frequency and photon energy) through microwave, infrared, visible, ultraviolet and X-ray to gamma. Photon energy rises with frequency, which is why the short-wavelength end is ionising.","reforge":{"stem":"Which electromagnetic radiation has the highest photon energy?","options":{"A":"Radio waves","B":"Infrared","C":"Ultraviolet","D":"Gamma rays"},"correct":"D"},"spec":"PHYS-3","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-phys-3.3"},{"id":"PHYS-N3-26","stem":"Light of wavelength 600 nm has a frequency of approximately:","options":{"A":"600 Hz","B":"1.8 × 10² Hz","C":"5.0 × 10¹⁴ Hz","D":"2.0 × 10⁻¹⁵ Hz"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GPHYS-WAVES","scaffold":"Rearranging c = fλ gives f = c ÷ λ = (3.00 × 10⁸) ÷ (600 × 10⁻⁹) = 5.0 × 10¹⁴ Hz. Converting nanometres to metres is essential: 1 nm = 10⁻⁹ m. Visible light spans roughly 400 nm (violet) to 700 nm (red), corresponding to frequencies around 4–7.5 × 10¹⁴ Hz, so any answer far outside that range signals a conversion error.","reforge":{"stem":"The energy of a photon is related to its wavelength by:","options":{"A":"E = hc/λ","B":"E = hλ","C":"E = λ/hc","D":"E = h/λc"},"correct":"A"},"spec":"PHYS-3","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-phys-3.3"},{"id":"PHYS-N3-27","stem":"A stationary wave on a string fixed at both ends has its fundamental frequency when the string length equals:","options":{"A":"A quarter of a wavelength","B":"One wavelength","C":"Two wavelengths","D":"Half a wavelength"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GPHYS-WAVES","scaffold":"With both ends fixed there must be a node at each end, so the fundamental has a single antinode and the string holds half a wavelength: λ = 2L. Harmonics follow at integer multiples of that frequency. For a pipe closed at one end there is a node at the closed end and an antinode at the open end, so the fundamental fits a quarter wavelength and only odd harmonics appear.","reforge":{"stem":"A pipe closed at one end produces which harmonics?","options":{"A":"All harmonics","B":"Only odd harmonics","C":"Only even harmonics","D":"No harmonics"},"correct":"B"},"spec":"PHYS-3","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-phys-3.3"},{"id":"PHYS-N3-28","stem":"Increasing the tension in a vibrating string raises the fundamental frequency because the wave speed:","options":{"A":"Increases","B":"Decreases","C":"Stays constant","D":"Becomes zero"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GPHYS-WAVES","scaffold":"Wave speed on a string is v = √(T/μ), where T is tension and μ mass per unit length. Since the fundamental wavelength is fixed at 2L, f = v/2L rises with tension and falls with a heavier string. This is why tightening a guitar string raises its pitch, and why the thicker strings sound lower at the same tension and length.","reforge":{"stem":"A thicker string of greater mass per unit length, at the same tension and length, produces a fundamental frequency that is:","options":{"A":"Higher","B":"Zero","C":"Lower","D":"The same"},"correct":"C"},"spec":"PHYS-3","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-phys-3.3"},{"id":"PHYS-N3-29","stem":"A converging lens forms a real image when the object is placed:","options":{"A":"At the focal point","B":"Beyond the focal length","C":"Between the lens and the focal point","D":"At the lens surface"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GPHYS-WAVES","scaffold":"A converging lens forms a real, inverted image when the object lies beyond the focal length; inside the focal length it forms a virtual, upright, magnified image, as in a magnifying glass. Real images can be projected onto a screen because the rays actually meet; virtual images cannot. The lens equation 1/f = 1/u + 1/v relates the distances, with sign conventions applied consistently.","reforge":{"stem":"A virtual image differs from a real image in that it:","options":{"A":"Forms only when using curved mirrors","B":"Is always inverted","C":"Is always smaller","D":"Cannot be projected onto a screen"},"correct":"D"},"spec":"PHYS-3","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-phys-3.3"},{"id":"PHYS-N3-30","stem":"The Doppler effect causes the observed frequency of a source moving away from an observer to be:","options":{"A":"Higher than emitted","B":"Zero","C":"Lower than emitted","D":"Unchanged"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GPHYS-ASTRONOMY","scaffold":"A receding source stretches the observed wavelength and lowers the frequency; an approaching source compresses it and raises the frequency. For light this appears as redshift and blueshift. The redshift of distant galaxies, increasing with distance, is the observational basis of Hubble's law and the evidence that the universe is expanding.","reforge":{"stem":"The redshift of distant galaxies increasing with distance is evidence that the universe is:","options":{"A":"Expanding","B":"Contracting","C":"Static","D":"Rotating"},"correct":"A"},"spec":"PHYS-3","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-phys-3.3"},{"id":"PHYS-N3-31","stem":"Hubble's law states that a galaxy's recession velocity is proportional to its:","options":{"A":"Apparent brightness","B":"Mass","C":"Temperature","D":"Distance from us"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GPHYS-ASTRONOMY","scaffold":"Hubble's law gives v = H₀d, so more distant galaxies recede faster. The reciprocal of the Hubble constant estimates the age of the universe. Together with the cosmic microwave background radiation and the observed abundance of light elements, this is the principal evidence for the Big Bang model. Uncertainty in H₀ is the main uncertainty in the age estimate.","reforge":{"stem":"The cosmic microwave background radiation is evidence for the Big Bang because it is:","options":{"A":"Emitted by nearby stars","B":"Radiation left from the hot early universe","C":"Produced by collisions between distant galaxies","D":"A form of visible light only"},"correct":"B"},"spec":"PHYS-3","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-phys-3.9"},{"id":"PHYS-N3-32","stem":"A star's luminosity is best described as the:","options":{"A":"Total power radiated by the star","B":"Brightness as it appears from Earth","C":"Surface temperature","D":"Distance from Earth"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GPHYS-ASTRONOMY","scaffold":"Luminosity is the total power output in watts, an intrinsic property. Apparent brightness depends additionally on distance, following an inverse square law, so a very luminous distant star can appear fainter than a nearby dim one. Stefan's law gives L = 4πr²σT⁴, so luminosity depends strongly on temperature, and Wien's law relates peak wavelength to temperature by λmaxT = constant.","reforge":{"stem":"Wien's displacement law states that a hotter star has a peak emission wavelength that is:","options":{"A":"Longer","B":"Zero","C":"Shorter","D":"Unchanged"},"correct":"C"},"spec":"PHYS-3","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-phys-3.9"},{"id":"PHYS-N3-33","stem":"On a Hertzsprung–Russell diagram, most stars lie along the:","options":{"A":"Supergiant branch","B":"Main sequence","C":"White dwarf region","D":"Horizontal axis only"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GPHYS-ASTRONOMY","scaffold":"The Hertzsprung–Russell diagram plots luminosity against temperature, with temperature increasing to the left. Most stars, including the Sun, spend the majority of their lives on the main sequence fusing hydrogen. Red giants and supergiants lie above and to the right, being cool but very luminous because they are large; white dwarfs lie below and to the left, hot but faint because they are small.","reforge":{"stem":"A red giant is very luminous despite its low surface temperature because it has a very large:","options":{"A":"Rotation rate","B":"Mass","C":"Density","D":"Radius"},"correct":"D"},"spec":"PHYS-3","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-phys-3.9"},{"id":"PHYS-N3-34","stem":"A star of mass similar to the Sun will end its life as a:","options":{"A":"Black hole","B":"Neutron star","C":"White dwarf","D":"Supernova remnant only"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GPHYS-ASTRONOMY","scaffold":"Low and intermediate mass stars swell to red giants, shed their outer layers as a planetary nebula and leave a white dwarf supported by electron degeneracy pressure, below the Chandrasekhar limit of about 1.4 solar masses. More massive stars end in a supernova, leaving a neutron star or, above roughly 3 solar masses of remnant core, a black hole. Mass at the end of life determines the outcome.","reforge":{"stem":"A stellar remnant more massive than about 3 solar masses is expected to become a:","options":{"A":"Black hole","B":"White dwarf","C":"Red giant","D":"Main sequence star"},"correct":"A"},"spec":"PHYS-3","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-phys-3.9"},{"id":"PHYS-N3-35","stem":"A telescope's resolving power is improved most directly by increasing the:","options":{"A":"Number of lenses used","B":"Magnification of the eyepiece lens","C":"Length of the tube","D":"Diameter of the objective aperture"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GPHYS-ASTRONOMY","scaffold":"Resolving power is limited by diffraction, following the Rayleigh criterion θ ≈ λ/D, so a larger aperture D gives finer resolution and also collects more light, revealing fainter objects. Increasing eyepiece magnification enlarges the image but cannot recover detail the aperture never resolved — this is why large telescopes are built with large mirrors rather than merely stronger eyepieces.","reforge":{"stem":"Increasing magnification beyond the resolving limit of a telescope produces an image that is:","options":{"A":"Sharper and far more detailed","B":"Larger but no more detailed","C":"Brighter and sharper","D":"Smaller but clearer"},"correct":"B"},"spec":"PHYS-3","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-phys-3.4"},{"id":"PHYS-N3-36","stem":"A current-carrying coil in a magnetic field experiences a turning effect. This is the principle of the:","options":{"A":"Electric motor","B":"Transformer core","C":"Capacitor","D":"Diode"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GPHYS-FIELDS-ELECTROMAGNETISM","scaffold":"Opposite sides of the coil carry current in opposite directions, so by F = BIL the forces on them act oppositely and produce a couple that rotates the coil — the motor effect. A split-ring commutator reverses the current every half turn to keep rotation continuous. A generator is the reverse process, using rotation to induce an emf by Faraday's law.","reforge":{"stem":"The purpose of a split-ring commutator in a d.c. motor is to:","options":{"A":"Reduce the current drawn","B":"Smooth the output voltage","C":"Reverse the current every half turn","D":"Increase the magnetic field strength"},"correct":"C"},"spec":"PHYS-3","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-phys-3.7"},{"id":"PHYS-N3-37","stem":"Magnetic flux through a coil is greatest when the plane of the coil is:","options":{"A":"Aligned parallel to the field","B":"Perpendicular to the field","C":"At 45° to the field","D":"Rotating rapidly"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GPHYS-FIELDS-ELECTROMAGNETISM","scaffold":"Flux Φ = BA cos θ, where θ is measured between the field and the normal to the coil. Flux is maximum when the field is along the normal — that is, when the plane of the coil is perpendicular to the field — and zero when the field lies in the plane of the coil. Careful reading of whether an angle is to the plane or to the normal avoids the commonest error here.","reforge":{"stem":"A coil is rotated in a uniform magnetic field. The induced emf is greatest when the flux is:","options":{"A":"Constant","B":"At its maximum possible value","C":"Zero and unchanging","D":"Changing most rapidly"},"correct":"D"},"spec":"PHYS-3","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-phys-3.3"},{"id":"PHYS-N3-38","stem":"An electron is accelerated through a pd of 100 V. Its gain in kinetic energy is:","options":{"A":"100 J","B":"6.3 × 10¹⁶ J","C":"1.6 × 10⁻¹⁷ J","D":"1.6 × 10⁻²¹ J"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GPHYS-QUANTUM-NUCLEAR","scaffold":"Energy gained = eV = 1.60 × 10⁻¹⁹ × 100 = 1.6 × 10⁻¹⁷ J, equivalently 100 eV. Setting this equal to ½mv² gives the final speed. The electronvolt is convenient precisely because the numerical value in eV equals the accelerating voltage, avoiding repeated multiplication by e until a final conversion to joules is needed.","reforge":{"stem":"One electronvolt is defined as the energy gained by an electron accelerated through:","options":{"A":"One volt","B":"One joule","C":"One coulomb","D":"One newton"},"correct":"A"},"spec":"PHYS-3","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-phys-3.4"},{"id":"PHYS-N3-39","stem":"In a discharge tube, an excited gas produces a line emission spectrum consisting of:","options":{"A":"No visible light at all","B":"A continuous band of all colours","C":"Dark lines against a bright background","D":"Bright lines at specific wavelengths"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GPHYS-QUANTUM-NUCLEAR","scaffold":"Electrons excited in a discharge tube fall back through discrete energy levels, emitting photons at specific wavelengths and producing bright lines on a dark background. An absorption spectrum shows the reverse: dark lines on a continuous background where a cool gas has absorbed those same wavelengths. Because the level structure is unique to each element, spectra identify elements — including in distant stars.","reforge":{"stem":"Dark lines in the Sun's spectrum are caused by cooler gases in its atmosphere that:","options":{"A":"Emit those wavelengths","B":"Absorb those wavelengths","C":"Reflect all wavelengths","D":"Produce a continuous spectrum"},"correct":"B"},"spec":"PHYS-3","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-phys-3.3"},{"id":"PHYS-N3-40","stem":"The activity of a radioactive source is measured in:","options":{"A":"Becquerels","B":"Grays","C":"Sieverts of dose","D":"Coulombs"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GPHYS-QUANTUM-NUCLEAR","scaffold":"Activity is measured in becquerels, one decay per second. Absorbed dose is measured in grays, one joule per kilogram of tissue, and equivalent dose in sieverts, which weights the absorbed dose by the type of radiation to reflect biological harm — alpha being far more damaging internally than gamma for the same absorbed energy. Distinguishing these three quantities is frequently tested.","reforge":{"stem":"Equivalent dose in sieverts differs from absorbed dose in grays because it accounts for the:","options":{"A":"Mass of the tissue","B":"Distance from the radioactive source","C":"Radiation type and its effect","D":"Half-life of the source"},"correct":"C"},"spec":"PHYS-3","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-phys-3.8"},{"id":"PHYS-N3-41","stem":"A student uses a light gate and timer to measure acceleration. The main advantage over a stopwatch is:","options":{"A":"It measures distance directly","B":"It removes reaction-time error","C":"It requires no calibration","D":"It eliminates all systematic error"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GPHYS-MEASUREMENT","scaffold":"Reaction time in starting and stopping a stopwatch introduces a random error of order 0.2 s, which is significant over short intervals. A light gate triggers electronically, removing it. It does not remove systematic errors such as a misaligned gate or an incorrectly measured card length. Reducing uncertainty further means taking repeats and using the largest practical distances and times.","reforge":{"stem":"Using a longer distance when measuring average speed reduces the:","options":{"A":"Need for any repeats","B":"Systematic error in the metre ruler","C":"True value of the speed","D":"Percentage uncertainty in timing"},"correct":"D"},"spec":"PHYS-3","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-phys-3.4"}]}});
